/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/sc-200.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("sc-200", [["sa1",0,1,"A legitimate admin tool on one server keeps raising the same Defender for Endpoint alert. The SOC wants to stop that alert only for that file on that server and keep all other protection. What should they create?",["An allow indicator for the file hash across the tenant","A device group with automation set to no response","Offboarding the server from Defender for Endpoint","An alert tuning rule scoped to that file and device"],3,"Alert tuning (suppression) rules hide or auto-resolve a specific benign alert pattern and leave the sensor running. An allow indicator is tempting, but it changes prevention for every device in the tenant, not just alerting on one server.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure settings in Microsoft Defender XDR",["An allow indicator changes prevention for that hash on every device in the tenant, which is far broader than silencing one alert on one server.","A device group with no automated response only changes how automated investigations remediate; the alert would still fire.","Offboarding removes all Defender for Endpoint protection and visibility from the server, which breaks the requirement to keep other protection.",null],2],["sa2",0,1,"The SOC manager wants an email every time Defender XDR creates a high-severity incident. What should be configured?",["The Security Operator role for the manager's account","An incident notification rule filtered to high severity","A Sentinel watchlist that holds the manager's email address","The unified audit log setting in Microsoft Purview"],1,"Incident notification rules in the Defender portal settings send email to chosen recipients for incidents that match filters such as severity. A role only grants portal access; it does not send email.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure settings in Microsoft Defender XDR",["The Security Operator role only grants permissions in the portal; roles never send email notifications.",null,"A watchlist is reference data queried in KQL inside Sentinel; storing an email address there does not send anything.","The unified audit log records user and admin activity for searches; it is not a notification mechanism for incidents."],1],["sa3",0,1,"Before enforcing the attack surface reduction rule that blocks credential theft from LSASS, the endpoint team wants to see which apps it would affect. What should they do first?",["Deploy the rule in audit mode and review the events it logs","Deploy the rule in block mode on the domain controllers","Turn on controlled folder access in block mode instead","Create a custom detection rule that copies the rule's ASR logic"],0,"Audit mode logs what the ASR rule would have blocked without breaking anything, so you can add exclusions before enforcing. Block mode on critical servers first is the risky choice this is meant to avoid.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",[null,"Block mode enforces the rule immediately and could break legitimate apps on critical servers, which is exactly what the team wants to find out first.","Controlled folder access protects folders from ransomware writes; it is a different feature and tells you nothing about the LSASS rule's impact.","A custom detection only raises alerts from hunting data and cannot reproduce or preview the ASR engine's blocking behavior."],2],["sa4",0,1,"An attacker with local admin rights tries to turn off Microsoft Defender Antivirus real-time protection through the registry. Which feature prevents this change?",["Network protection","Controlled folder access","Tamper protection","Device discovery"],2,"Tamper protection locks security settings such as real-time protection so they cannot be changed locally, even by admins. Network protection blocks connections to bad domains; it does not protect settings.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",["Network protection blocks outbound connections to malicious domains and IPs; it does not lock antivirus settings.","Controlled folder access stops untrusted apps writing to protected folders, not changes to Defender's own configuration.",null,"Device discovery finds unmanaged devices on the network; it has no role in protecting security settings."],1],["sa5",0,1,"Tier-1 analysts in the EU office should see and act on EU endpoints only in Defender for Endpoint. What should the administrator do?",["Create an EU device group and give only the analysts' group access to it","Tag the EU devices and give the analysts the Security Reader role in Entra","Build a separate Log Analytics workspace for EU devices","Add a Conditional Access policy that blocks non-EU pages"],0,"With Defender XDR RBAC, device groups limit which devices a user group can see and manage. An Entra role such as Security Reader applies to the whole tenant, so tags alone do not restrict it.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",[null,"Security Reader is a tenant-wide Entra role, so tags alone would not limit the analysts to EU devices, and Reader cannot take response actions.","Defender for Endpoint data is not scoped by Log Analytics workspaces; a separate workspace would not restrict the Defender portal.","Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions to apps, not which devices a user can see inside the Defender portal."],2],["sa6",0,1,"A company runs workloads on Azure VMs and on AWS EC2 and wants Defender for Cloud server threat detection on both. What should be done?",["Install the Defender for Identity sensor on each EC2 instance","Connect the AWS account to Defender for Cloud and enable Defender for Servers","Rely on foundational CSPM, which adds server threat alerts","Forward EC2 syslog to Sentinel through the Syslog connector"],1,"Defender for Cloud uses a multicloud connector for AWS, and the Defender for Servers plan adds workload threat protection, including Defender for Endpoint. Foundational CSPM only gives posture recommendations and secure score, not threat alerts.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["The Defender for Identity sensor is for domain controllers and identity servers such as AD FS and AD CS, not for EC2 workload protection.",null,"Foundational CSPM gives free posture recommendations and secure score only; it does not add server threat detection.","Forwarding syslog to Sentinel gives raw logs for your own rules but does not enable Defender for Cloud's server threat protection."],2],["sa7",0,1,"Which Defender for Cloud capability gives secure score and basic security recommendations at no extra charge?",["Defender CSPM","Defender for Servers Plan 2","Foundational CSPM","Defender for Storage"],2,"Foundational CSPM is on by default and free. Defender CSPM adds paid features such as attack path analysis, and the Defender for plans are paid workload protections.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Defender CSPM is the paid posture plan that adds features such as attack path analysis and the cloud security explorer.","Defender for Servers Plan 2 is a paid workload protection plan for machines, not the free secure score baseline.",null,"Defender for Storage is a paid plan that detects threats against storage accounts, not the free posture baseline."],1],["sa8",0,1,"An analyst must be able to assign, comment on and close Sentinel incidents but must not create or edit analytics rules. Which built-in role fits?",["Microsoft Sentinel Reader","Microsoft Sentinel Contributor","Log Analytics Reader","Microsoft Sentinel Responder"],3,"Responder can manage incidents (assign, change status, close) but cannot create or change content. Contributor can also edit rules, which is more than needed, and Reader cannot change incidents.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["Sentinel Reader can view incidents and content but cannot assign, change or close incidents.","Sentinel Contributor can also create and edit analytics rules and other content, which exceeds what this analyst should have.","Log Analytics Reader only allows reading workspace data and gives no rights to manage Sentinel incidents.",null],2],["sa9",0,1,"An automation rule should run a playbook, but the playbook's resource group appears greyed out when you pick it. What is the most likely fix?",["Move the playbook into the Log Analytics workspace itself","Grant Sentinel the Automation Contributor role on that resource group","Give the analyst the Logic App Contributor role on the whole subscription","Re-create the playbook with an alert trigger rather than incident"],1,"Sentinel needs explicit permission (Microsoft Sentinel Automation Contributor) on the playbook's resource group before automation rules can run playbooks there. The analyst's own Logic App rights do not give Sentinel's service account that permission.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure automation",["Playbooks are Logic Apps that live in a resource group; they cannot be moved inside a Log Analytics workspace.",null,"The analyst's own Logic App rights do not give the Sentinel service account permission to run playbooks in that resource group.","The trigger type affects which playbooks can be run, but the greyed-out resource group is a permissions problem, not a trigger problem."],3],["sa10",0,1,"A firewall sends logs in Common Event Format. How should they reach Sentinel today?",["Through a Linux forwarder using the CEF via AMA connector and a DCR","Through the legacy Log Analytics agent installed directly on the firewall","Through the Windows Security Events via AMA connector","Straight from the firewall to the workspace with no forwarder"],0,"Appliances that can't run an agent send CEF over syslog to a Linux forwarder running the Azure Monitor Agent, and a data collection rule sends the events to the CommonSecurityLog table. The legacy Log Analytics agent (MMA) is retired.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",[null,"The legacy Log Analytics agent (MMA) is retired, and firewalls generally cannot run an agent at all.","Windows Security Events via AMA collects Windows event logs from Windows machines, not CEF over syslog from appliances.","Firewalls cannot write directly to a Log Analytics workspace; CEF needs a forwarder with the Azure Monitor Agent and a DCR."],1],["sa11",0,1,"Ingestion costs from domain controllers are too high. The SOC only needs a small set of specific Windows event IDs. What is the best approach?",["The All Security Events set, then filtering later inside analytics rules","A watchlist of the needed event IDs linked to the connector","A data collection rule with a custom XPath filter for those event IDs","Workspace retention lowered to 30 days to offset ingestion"],2,"DCRs filter events at the source, so you pay only for what you collect. Filtering inside analytics rules still ingests and bills every event, and lowering retention does not reduce ingestion charges.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["Collecting All Security Events ingests and bills every event; filtering later in rules does not reduce ingestion cost.","Watchlists are reference data for queries and cannot be linked to a connector to filter what gets collected.",null,"Retention is billed separately from ingestion, so shortening retention does not cut the ingestion charges that are the problem."],2],["sa12",0,1,"A team wants the data connector, analytics rules, workbooks and hunting queries for a third-party product installed together. Where should they go?",["The Community tab of the Sentinel GitHub repository only","The Analytics rule templates page, one template at a time","The Data connectors page of the Azure Monitor portal","The product's solution in the Sentinel Content hub"],3,"Content hub solutions package connectors, rules, workbooks, parsers and hunting queries so they can be installed and updated as one unit. Adding templates one by one misses the connector and workbook.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["The GitHub repository hosts community content, but installing from it manually does not give a packaged, updatable solution with its connector.","Adding rule templates one at a time misses the connector, workbooks and hunting queries that ship with the product.","Azure Monitor's own pages manage agents and DCRs; they do not install Sentinel content packages.",null],1],["sa13",0,1,"Developers want an in-house application to send JSON security events to a custom Sentinel table over HTTPS. Which method should they use?",["The HTTP Data Collector API with a shared workspace key","The Logs Ingestion API with a data collection rule and a custom table","A Syslog via AMA connector on the application server","Windows Event Forwarding to a collector server"],1,"The Logs Ingestion API sends data through a DCR, which can transform it, into a custom table using Entra ID authentication. The older HTTP Data Collector API is being retired and relies on a shared key.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["The HTTP Data Collector API is the legacy method that uses a shared workspace key and is being retired.",null,"Syslog via AMA collects syslog messages from Linux hosts; it is not an HTTPS API for an application to post JSON.","Windows Event Forwarding moves Windows event logs between servers and does not accept custom JSON from an application."],2],["sa14",0,1,"Why should a scheduled analytics rule map entities such as Account, Host and IP?",["So the query runs faster against the analytics tier","So the rule can run as a near-real-time rule","So alerts carry structured entities for pivoting, grouping and UEBA","So Sentinel can skip ingesting unmapped columns"],2,"Entity mapping turns query columns into entities that drive the investigation graph, entity pages, alert grouping and correlation. It does not change query speed or what is ingested.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Entity mapping happens after the query returns results; it does not make the query run any faster.","NRT is a separate rule type chosen when the rule is created; entity mapping does not change the rule type.",null,"Entity mapping has no effect on ingestion; every column in the table is still stored whether it is mapped or not."],1],["sa15",0,1,"A detection for adding a user to Domain Admins must raise an alert within about a minute of the event arriving. Which rule type fits best?",["A near-real-time (NRT) analytics rule","A scheduled rule set to run every five minutes","An anomaly rule based on machine learning","A Fusion multistage attack rule"],0,"NRT rules run about once a minute on newly arrived data, which suits simple, high-value events. Scheduled rules can't run more often than every five minutes, and anomaly and Fusion rules aren't built for a specific single event.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",[null,"Scheduled rules run at most every five minutes and usually add ingestion delay, so they cannot reliably alert within about a minute.","Anomaly rules use machine learning baselines over time and are not designed to fire on a single specific event.","Fusion correlates multiple alerts into multistage incidents; it is not a rule you write to catch one specific event quickly."],2],["sa16",0,1,"A brute-force rule creates hundreds of incidents a day, one for every alert about the same user. What reduces this without losing alerts?",["A higher rule threshold, so that most of the alerts no longer fire","An automation rule that closes every new incident","A switch from a scheduled rule to an NRT rule","Alert grouping by matching entities within a time window"],3,"Alert grouping adds related alerts (for example, the same account within 24 hours) to one incident, so nothing is lost. Raising the threshold or auto-closing incidents hides real detections.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Raising the threshold suppresses real detections instead of consolidating them, so alerts are lost.","Automatically closing every incident hides genuine attacks and defeats the purpose of the rule.","Switching to NRT changes how often the rule runs but does nothing to group related alerts into one incident.",null],2],["sa17",0,1,"After turning on the Microsoft Defender XDR connector with incident integration in Sentinel, the team sees duplicate incidents for the same Defender alerts. What should they do?",["Delete the Defender XDR connector and use the older product connectors","Turn off Microsoft incident creation rules for the Defender products","Add an automation rule that closes incidents from Defender","Lower the severity of all Defender alerts in Sentinel"],1,"With incident integration, Defender XDR incidents sync into Sentinel. Microsoft incident creation rules for the same products create a second copy, so they should be turned off (the connector offers to do this).","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["The Defender XDR connector with incident integration is the recommended approach; going back to older connectors loses correlation and sync.",null,"Auto-closing Defender incidents hides the synced copies too and treats the symptom rather than removing the duplicate source.","Changing severity does not stop two incidents from being created for the same alerts."],3],["sa18",0,1,"Which columns must a Defender XDR advanced hunting query return before it can be saved as a custom detection rule?",["Only Timestamp and a Severity value calculated in the query","A column named AlertTitle and one named MitreTechnique","Timestamp, an event ID such as ReportId, and an entity column such as DeviceId","Any columns, as long as the query uses summarize"],2,"Custom detections need the event time, a column that identifies the event record and at least one impacted-entity column so alerts can be linked to devices, users or mailboxes. Severity and MITRE techniques are set in the rule wizard, not in query columns.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Severity is chosen in the rule wizard, and Timestamp alone is not enough; the query also needs a record ID and an entity column.","AlertTitle and MITRE techniques are entered in the wizard's alert details, not returned as query columns.",null,"summarize is not required, and a query that summarizes away the required columns cannot be saved as a detection."],1],["sa19",0,1,"Incidents from one specific analytics rule should be assigned to the Tier-2 queue and have their severity raised automatically. What is the simplest solution?",["An automation rule that runs on incident creation","A playbook with an alert trigger that calls the Graph API","A change to the rule's KQL that adds an Owner column","A workbook that highlights incidents from that rule"],0,"Automation rules can set the owner, severity, status, tags and tasks with no code. A playbook could do it too, but it adds cost and complexity for something built in.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure automation",[null,"A playbook could do this but adds a Logic App, cost and code for something automation rules do natively.","Adding an Owner column to the query output does not assign the incident to anyone in Sentinel.","A workbook only visualizes data; it cannot change an incident's owner or severity."],2],["sa20",0,1,"When a new incident is created, the SOC wants to look up each IP on a reputation service and post the results to a Teams channel. What should be built?",["An automation rule on its own, using a built-in 'post message' condition","A watchlist refreshed from the reputation service","A scheduled analytics rule that queries the reputation site","A playbook run by an automation rule when an incident is created"],3,"Calling external APIs and posting to Teams needs Logic Apps connectors, which is what playbooks provide. The automation rule is the trigger that runs the playbook.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure automation",["Automation rules have no built-in action to call external APIs or post to Teams; they can only run a playbook for that.","A watchlist stores reference data and cannot post enrichment results to Teams when an incident is created.","An analytics rule queries data in the workspace; it cannot call an external reputation website or post messages.",null],3],["sa21",0,1,"Analysts need a list of VIP accounts that several analytics rules can reference and that HR can update from a CSV. What should be used?",["A custom table filled through the Logs Ingestion API","A hard-coded dynamic() list inside each rule","A Sentinel watchlist queried with _GetWatchlist()","A Defender XDR device tag named VIP"],2,"Watchlists hold reference data, are easy to update from CSV, and are read in KQL with _GetWatchlist. Hard-coding the list in each rule means editing every rule whenever it changes.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["A custom table via the Logs Ingestion API is heavier to build and maintain, and HR could not simply upload a CSV to update it.","Hard-coding a dynamic() list in each rule means editing every rule whenever the VIP list changes.",null,"Device tags label devices in Defender XDR; they cannot hold a list of user accounts for Sentinel rules to reference."],2],["sa22",0,1,"The SOC lead wants to see which MITRE ATT&CK techniques are covered by active analytics rules and which have no coverage. Where should they look?",["The Threat analytics page in Defender XDR","The MITRE ATT&CK page in Microsoft Sentinel","The Secure score page in Defender for Cloud","The Content hub filtered by vendor"],1,"Sentinel's MITRE ATT&CK view shows coverage by active and available rules for each technique. Threat analytics covers threat campaigns, not your rule coverage.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Threat analytics covers Microsoft's reports on threat campaigns and your exposure, not which techniques your own rules cover.",null,"Secure score in Defender for Cloud measures configuration posture, not MITRE ATT&CK detection coverage.","Filtering Content hub by vendor shows installable solutions, not a coverage map of your active rules by technique."],1],["sa23",0,1,"Which Sentinel feature recommends actions such as cutting ingestion of data no rule uses or adding detections for gaps against common attack scenarios?",["UEBA","Workbooks","Livestream","SOC optimization"],3,"SOC optimization analyzes your ingestion and detections and suggests cost and coverage improvements. Workbooks only show whatever data you query.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["UEBA builds behavioral baselines for users and entities; it does not recommend ingestion or detection changes.","Workbooks display whatever queries you build; they do not analyze your SOC and suggest improvements.","Livestream runs a hunting query continuously against new data; it makes no cost or coverage recommendations.",null],1],["sa24",0,1,"High-volume network flow logs must be kept for a year for occasional investigations, but no analytics rule needs them. What is the most cost-effective option?",["Store them in the Sentinel data lake tier and query them when needed","Keep them in the analytics tier with one year of interactive retention","Load them into a watchlist so analysts can search them","Ingest them daily, then delete them after 30 days"],0,"The data lake tier is built for cheap long-term storage of high-volume data queried now and then. The analytics tier is the expensive choice here, since no detection needs the data.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",[null,"A year of interactive analytics retention for high-volume logs is the most expensive option, and no detection needs that speed.","Watchlists are small reference lists with size limits, not a store for high-volume flow logs.","Deleting the logs after 30 days fails the requirement to keep them for a year."],3],["sa25",0,1,"Which Sentinel feature builds interactive dashboards of ingestion volume, incident trends and SOC metrics from KQL queries?",["Workbooks","Watchlists","Notebooks","Automation rules"],0,"Workbooks are Azure Monitor dashboards driven by KQL. Notebooks are Jupyter environments for deeper analysis, not dashboards.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",[null,"Watchlists hold reference data such as VIP lists; they do not render dashboards.","Notebooks are Jupyter environments for deep analysis with Python, not interactive KQL dashboards for SOC metrics.","Automation rules act on incidents (assign, tag, run playbooks); they produce no visualizations."],1],["sa26",0,1,"The data team wants alerts on SQL injection attempts against an Azure SQL database. Which plan should be turned on?",["Defender for Storage","Defender for Databases (Defender for Azure SQL)","Defender CSPM","Defender for App Service"],1,"The database plan detects threats such as SQL injection and unusual access to Azure SQL. Defender for Storage watches storage accounts, not SQL queries.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Defender for Storage detects threats against storage accounts, such as malware uploads, not SQL injection.",null,"Defender CSPM adds posture features like attack path analysis; it does not raise SQL injection threat alerts.","Defender for App Service protects web apps hosted in App Service, not the Azure SQL database itself."],1],["sa27",0,1,"Where must the Defender for Identity sensor be installed to monitor on-premises Active Directory authentication?",["On every member workstation in the domain","On the Microsoft Entra Connect server only, not the DCs","On domain controllers and AD FS or AD CS servers","On a Linux syslog forwarder in the DMZ"],2,"The sensor reads authentication traffic and events directly on domain controllers (and identity servers such as AD FS and AD CS). Putting it only on the Entra Connect server misses DC traffic.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Workstations are covered by Defender for Endpoint; the Defender for Identity sensor belongs on identity infrastructure.","The Entra Connect server can have a sensor, but installing only there misses the authentication traffic on domain controllers.",null,"The sensor runs on Windows identity servers and reads traffic locally; a Linux syslog forwarder is not a supported placement."],1],["sa28",0,1,"Users sometimes click links in email that were safe at delivery but later start hosting a phishing page. Which Defender for Office 365 feature protects them?",["Safe Attachments dynamic delivery","Zero-hour auto purge for malware only","Anti-spam outbound policy","Safe Links time-of-click URL checks"],3,"Safe Links rewrites URLs and checks them again when the user clicks, so it catches links that turn malicious after delivery. Safe Attachments detonates attachments, not links.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",["Safe Attachments detonates attachments in a sandbox; dynamic delivery handles attachments, not links in the message body.","Zero-hour auto purge can remove messages after delivery, but limiting it to malware does not protect a user clicking a link that turned malicious.","Outbound anti-spam policies control mail your users send, not inbound links they click.",null],2],["sa29",0,1,"What is the easiest way to onboard hundreds of Azure Windows and Linux VMs to Defender for Endpoint?",["Run the local onboarding script on each VM by hand","Install the Defender for Identity sensor on each VM","Enable Defender for Servers, which deploys the Defender for Endpoint integration","Connect the VMs through the Windows Security Events connector"],2,"Defender for Servers includes Defender for Endpoint and can deploy the sensor to supported machines automatically. Local scripts work but don't scale.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Running the local script on each VM works but does not scale to hundreds of machines and is error-prone.","The Defender for Identity sensor monitors Active Directory on DCs; it does not onboard VMs to Defender for Endpoint.",null,"The Windows Security Events connector sends event logs to Sentinel; it does not onboard machines to Defender for Endpoint and ignores Linux."],2],["sa30",0,1,"The SOC suspects there are unmanaged laptops and IoT devices on the corporate network. Which Defender for Endpoint feature can find them using onboarded devices?",["Tamper protection","Live response","Attack surface reduction","Device discovery"],3,"Device discovery uses onboarded endpoints to find unmanaged devices on the same networks, so they can be onboarded or assessed. Live response is a remote shell on devices you already manage.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Tamper protection locks Defender settings on managed devices; it does not find unmanaged ones.","Live response is a remote shell on devices that are already onboarded, not a discovery tool.","Attack surface reduction rules block risky behaviors on managed endpoints and do not inventory unknown devices.",null],2],["sa31",0,1,"The SOC wants to block all endpoints from reaching a known command-and-control domain. What must be in place for a domain indicator with a Block action to work?",["Tamper protection turned off","Network protection turned on in block mode","Controlled folder access turned on in audit mode","A Sentinel TI data connector"],1,"URL and domain indicators are enforced through network protection (and SmartScreen in Edge). Without network protection, other browsers and processes aren't blocked.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",["Turning tamper protection off weakens security and has nothing to do with enforcing domain indicators.",null,"Controlled folder access protects folders from untrusted writes; audit mode would not block anything anyway.","A Sentinel TI connector brings indicators into Sentinel for matching, but it does not enforce blocking on endpoints."],3],["sa32",0,1,"Analysts want to work Sentinel incidents and Defender XDR incidents in one queue in the Defender portal. What is required?",["Onboard the Sentinel workspace to the Defender portal","Export Sentinel incidents into a Defender watchlist","Assign every analyst the Global Administrator role","Build a Logic App that copies incidents between portals"],0,"Connecting the workspace to the Defender portal (the unified security operations platform) puts Sentinel and Defender XDR incidents in one queue with shared hunting. Global Administrator is too broad and doesn't connect anything.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",[null,"Watchlists hold reference data; exporting incidents into one does not create a shared incident queue.","Global Administrator is excessive privilege and does not connect Sentinel to the Defender portal.","A custom Logic App copying incidents is fragile and unnecessary when the workspace can be onboarded to the unified platform."],2],["sa33",0,1,"Analysts want each user's sign-ins compared with that user's normal behavior and with peers, shown as insights on entity pages. What must be turned on?",["The Fusion rule with all alert sources","User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) with its data sources","A workbook built on the SigninLogs table","Defender for Cloud Apps session policies"],1,"UEBA builds baselines from sources such as sign-in and audit logs and adds anomalies and peer comparisons to entity pages. Fusion correlates alerts; it doesn't build per-user baselines.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["Fusion correlates alerts into multistage incidents; it does not build per-user baselines or peer comparisons.",null,"A workbook on SigninLogs can chart sign-ins but does not calculate behavioral baselines or add insights to entity pages.","Session policies in Defender for Cloud Apps control live sessions to apps; they do not add UEBA insights in Sentinel."],2],["sa34",0,1,"A partner shares threat indicators through a STIX/TAXII 2.x server. How should Sentinel ingest them?",["The Syslog via AMA connector","A weekly CSV upload into a Sentinel watchlist","The Defender for Cloud Apps connector","The Threat Intelligence TAXII data connector"],3,"The TAXII connector polls STIX feeds and stores the indicators as threat intelligence objects that rules and hunting can match. A weekly watchlist upload goes stale and loses the STIX details.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Syslog via AMA collects syslog messages from Linux hosts; it cannot poll a STIX/TAXII server.","A weekly CSV watchlist upload is manual, goes stale and discards the STIX object details.","The Defender for Cloud Apps connector brings in cloud app alerts and logs, not partner threat intelligence feeds.",null],2],["sa35",0,1,"Verbose proxy logs sit in a low-cost tier. The SOC wants an hourly aggregate of bytes per user in the analytics tier for dashboards and detections. What should they create?",["A near-real-time rule on the low-cost tier","A watchlist refreshed every hour","A summary rule that writes aggregated results to an analytics table","A workbook with auto-refresh turned on"],2,"Summary rules run a KQL aggregation on a schedule and save the smaller result in an analytics-tier table, which cuts cost while keeping the data useful. A workbook only displays data; it doesn't store results for rules.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["NRT rules run against analytics-tier data every minute and do not write aggregated results to a table.","A watchlist is manually maintained reference data, not a scheduled aggregation of proxy logs.",null,"Auto-refresh only redraws a dashboard; it does not store results that analytics rules can query."],3],["sa36",0,1,"Which Sentinel analytics rule type correlates low-fidelity alerts and anomalies from several products into high-fidelity multistage attack incidents?",["Fusion","Near-real-time (NRT)","Microsoft incident creation","Anomaly"],0,"Fusion uses machine learning to correlate signals across products into multistage incidents and is on by default. Anomaly rules produce anomaly records on their own and are one of the inputs Fusion can use.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",[null,"NRT rules run a single KQL query about every minute; they do not correlate alerts across products.","Microsoft incident creation rules turn alerts from Microsoft products into incidents one for one, without multistage correlation.","Anomaly rules produce individual anomaly records; they are an input Fusion can use, not the correlation engine itself."],1],["sa37",0,1,"Analysts want the suspicious command line shown on each alert from a scheduled rule so they can filter and triage without opening raw logs. What should the rule author configure?",["A File entity mapped to the CommandLine column value","Custom details that surface the CommandLine column","A watchlist containing the command lines seen so far","Alert grouping that groups incidents by the command line"],1,"Custom details copy chosen query columns into the alert so they appear on the alert and incident. A File entity describes a file, not a command line, so mapping the command line to it is misleading.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["A File entity describes a file, and forcing a command line into it produces misleading entities rather than a readable field.",null,"A watchlist of past command lines does not put the current alert's command line on the alert.","Alert grouping controls how alerts form incidents; it does not show the command line on each alert."],2],["sa38",0,2,"A Defender XDR incident links alerts from email, a laptop and an identity. The analyst wants the sequence of events and assets on one page. Where should they look?",["The device's software inventory","The Secure score page","The incident's attack story","The Action center history tab"],2,"The attack story shows correlated alerts in order with an incident graph of the affected assets. The software inventory lists installed programs, not the attack sequence.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Software inventory lists installed programs on one device, not the correlated sequence of events across assets.","Secure score measures security posture; it contains no incident timeline.",null,"The Action center history shows remediation actions that ran, not the full attack sequence and affected assets."],1],["sa39",0,2,"A laptop is beaconing to a command-and-control server. The analyst must stop lateral movement but keep collecting telemetry and running response actions. Which action fits?",["Isolate the device","Restrict app execution","Offboard the device","Run a quick antivirus scan"],0,"Isolation blocks all network traffic except the connection to the Defender service, so investigation and response can continue. Offboarding would remove your visibility.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",[null,"Restrict app execution blocks unsigned code but leaves network connectivity up, so lateral movement over the network is still possible.","Offboarding removes the sensor, so you lose the telemetry and response actions you need to keep.","An antivirus scan may find malware but does nothing to stop the device communicating with other hosts."],3],["sa40",0,2,"The incident responder needs autoruns, running processes, network connections and event logs from a remote device in one download. Which action should they use?",["Run antivirus scan","Initiate automated investigation","Restrict app execution","Collect investigation package"],3,"The investigation package gathers forensic data such as processes, services, scheduled tasks, network connections and logs into a zip file. A scan only looks for malware.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["An antivirus scan looks for malware and returns detections, not a forensic bundle of processes, autoruns and logs.","An automated investigation analyzes and remediates on its own; it does not give the responder a downloadable forensic package.","Restrict app execution limits which code can run; it collects no data.",null],2],["sa41",0,2,"An analyst needs to pull a suspicious DLL from a remote endpoint for analysis and list its scheduled tasks. Which capability allows this?",["A live response session with commands such as getfile","The file page's 'Download file' button, which works in all cases","Threat Explorer with the file hash","An advanced hunting query on DeviceFileEvents"],0,"Live response gives a remote shell for collecting files, running approved scripts and checking state. Advanced hunting shows events about the file but can't retrieve it.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",[null,"Download file on the file page depends on the file having been collected and is not always available; it also cannot list scheduled tasks.","Threat Explorer is a Defender for Office 365 tool for email; it cannot retrieve files from an endpoint.","Advanced hunting shows events about the file but cannot pull the file itself or run commands on the device."],2],["sa42",0,2,"A malicious executable is running on several devices. The analyst wants to kill its processes and quarantine it wherever it is found. Which action should they take from the file page?",["Add the file to an alert tuning rule","Stop and quarantine file","Collect investigation package on each device","Submit the file for deep analysis only"],1,"Stop and quarantine file ends running processes and quarantines the file on devices where it is found. Deep analysis gives information but doesn't contain anything.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["Alert tuning only hides or resolves alerts; the malware would keep running.",null,"Collecting investigation packages gathers forensic data but does not kill processes or quarantine the file.","Deep analysis detonates the file for a report; it contains nothing on the affected devices."],2],["sa43",0,2,"A finance user's laptop runs an unsigned hacking tool, but the user must keep working on email and Teams during the investigation. Which action fits?",["Isolate the device from the network","Offboard the device and re-image it straight away","Turn off tamper protection to allow cleanup","Restrict app execution to Microsoft-signed code"],3,"Restrict app execution stops unsigned code while the network stays up, so Microsoft-signed apps keep working. Isolation would cut the user off completely.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["Isolation cuts all network traffic except to Defender, so the user could not keep using email and Teams.","Offboarding and re-imaging immediately destroys evidence and removes visibility before the investigation is done.","Turning off tamper protection weakens the device's defenses and does not stop the hacking tool.",null],3],["sa44",0,2,"A phishing email reached 300 mailboxes before it was detected. How should the analyst remove the copies?",["Ask each user to delete the email and empty their trash","Block the sender and wait for zero-hour auto purge","Find the messages in Threat Explorer and take a soft-delete action","Create a mail flow rule, which only affects messages sent in future"],2,"Threat Explorer (or advanced hunting) can find every delivered copy and remove them all at once, with the action tracked in the Action center. Blocking the sender affects only future mail.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",["Relying on 300 users to delete the email is slow, unreliable and leaves copies behind.","Blocking the sender only affects future mail, and ZAP may not act on messages it does not reclassify.",null,"Mail flow rules act on messages in transit, so they cannot remove copies already delivered to mailboxes."],2],["sa45",0,2,"Where does an analyst review emails that users flagged with the built-in Report button?",["The User reported tab on the Submissions page","The Quarantine page under Review","The reports page for Attack simulation training","The Purview content search results"],0,"User-reported messages appear on the Submissions page, where analysts can triage them and send them to Microsoft. Quarantine holds messages the filters caught, not ones users reported.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",[null,"Quarantine holds messages that filtering policies caught, not messages users reported with the Report button.","Attack simulation training reports cover simulated phishing campaigns, not real messages users reported.","Purview content search finds content for eDiscovery; it is not the triage queue for user-reported messages."],1],["sa46",0,2,"Defender for Identity raises 'Suspected DCSync attack' from a workstation. What does this alert mean?",["A workstation's clock is out of sync with the domain controller","A non-DC host asked a domain controller to replicate directory data, such as password hashes","A user changed their password on a domain-joined device","Group Policy failed to replicate between domain controllers"],1,"DCSync abuses replication rights to pull credential material. Only domain controllers should replicate, so a request from a workstation strongly suggests credential theft. It has nothing to do with time sync.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Identity",["DCSync has nothing to do with clock time; it abuses directory replication to steal credentials.",null,"A normal password change is routine and does not involve a workstation requesting replication from a DC.","Replication failures between DCs are an operational issue; DCSync is a non-DC impersonating a DC to pull secrets."],1],["sa47",0,2,"The analyst wants to see how compromising a help desk workstation could let an attacker reach a Domain Admin account through saved sessions and local admin rights. Which Defender for Identity feature shows this?",["Honeytoken alerts","Entity tags","Lateral movement paths","Sensor health"],2,"Lateral movement paths map how attackers could hop through machines and credentials to reach sensitive accounts. Honeytokens are decoy accounts; they don't map paths.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Identity",["Honeytoken alerts fire when decoy accounts are used; they do not map paths to sensitive accounts.","Entity tags mark accounts or devices as sensitive but do not show how an attacker could reach them.",null,"Sensor health reports on Defender for Identity sensor status, not attack paths."],2],["sa48",0,2,"During a human-operated ransomware attack, Defender XDR contained a compromised user account and several devices by itself within minutes. Which capability did this?",["A Sentinel automation rule on incident creation","A custom detection rule","Attack simulation training","Automatic attack disruption"],3,"Automatic attack disruption uses high-confidence XDR signals to contain compromised users and devices automatically. Custom detections raise alerts; they don't contain anything on their own.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Sentinel automation rules act on incidents you configure; they are not the built-in XDR capability that contained this attack.","Custom detection rules raise alerts from your queries and can take configured actions, but they did not provide this built-in, signal-driven containment.","Attack simulation training runs phishing simulations for users; it does not contain real attacks.",null],1],["sa49",0,2,"The SOC has confirmed a user's credentials were stolen and wants Entra ID Protection risk policies to treat the user as high risk. Which action should they take?",["Dismiss user risk","Add the user to a watchlist","Confirm user compromised","Disable UEBA for the user"],2,"'Confirm user compromised' sets the user's risk to high, which triggers risk-based Conditional Access and helps train the detections. Dismissing risk does the opposite.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to Microsoft Entra ID risks",["Dismissing user risk sets the risk to none, which is the opposite of what the SOC wants.","Watchlists are Sentinel reference data and have no effect on Entra ID Protection risk levels.",null,"Disabling UEBA removes behavioral insights in Sentinel and does not raise the user's Entra risk."],2],["sa50",0,2,"An investigation finds an attacker created an inbox rule that forwards a manager's mail to an outside address. Besides resetting the password, what should the responder do?",["Only turn on MFA, since the rule expires on its own","Leave the rule so the attacker isn't alerted","Delete the mailbox and restore it from backup","Remove the rule and revoke the user's sessions"],3,"Inbox rules persist after a password reset, and existing sessions can stay valid. Removing the rule and revoking refresh tokens both remove the attacker's access.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",["Turning on MFA does not remove the rule, and inbox rules do not expire; mail would keep forwarding.","Leaving the rule in place keeps leaking the manager's mail to the attacker.","Deleting and restoring the mailbox is disruptive and unnecessary when the malicious rule can simply be removed.",null],3],["sa51",0,2,"A user reports dozens of MFA push prompts at night that they didn't start. What should the analyst do first?",["Tell the user to approve one prompt to stop the notifications","Reset the password and revoke sessions, since the password is likely known","Turn off MFA for the user until the prompts stop","Wait for Identity Protection to raise the risk level"],1,"Repeated prompts mean someone already has the password and is attempting MFA fatigue. Resetting and revoking stops them. Approving a prompt would give the attacker access.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to Microsoft Entra ID risks",["Approving a prompt would complete the attacker's sign-in and hand them access to the account.",null,"Turning off MFA removes the last barrier protecting an account whose password is already known.","Waiting leaves the account exposed while the attacker keeps trying; the user report is enough to act on."],2],["sa52",0,2,"Defender for Cloud Apps flags a newly consented OAuth app with mail read-write permissions that is sending unusual volumes of email. What is the best containment step?",["Ban or revoke the app so its consent and tokens stop working","Reset the passwords of users who consented","Add the app's publisher to a watchlist","Increase the anomaly policy threshold"],0,"OAuth apps use their own tokens, so a password reset doesn't remove their access. Banning or revoking the app removes it. Changing thresholds only hides alerts.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"OAuth apps keep their own tokens and consent, so password resets do not remove the app's access.","A watchlist entry for the publisher only helps queries; it does not stop the app sending mail.","Raising the threshold hides the alert without stopping the malicious app."],3],["sa53",0,2,"The SOC wants Microsoft Purview DLP alerts triaged in the same place as endpoint and identity alerts. Where can analysts do this?",["The Entra admin center sign-in logs","The Azure Activity log","The Intune device compliance report","The Defender portal incident queue"],3,"Purview DLP alerts integrate with Defender XDR incidents, so they can be correlated with other signals in one queue. Sign-in logs don't contain DLP alerts.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate data loss prevention and insider risk alerts",["Entra sign-in logs show authentication events, not Purview DLP alerts.","The Azure Activity log records Azure resource management operations, not DLP policy matches.","Intune compliance reports show device compliance state, not DLP alerts or incidents.",null],2],["sa54",0,2,"In a Defender for Cloud security alert for a suspicious process on an Azure VM, which part lists mitigation steps and lets you trigger a Logic App or create a suppression rule?",["The Take action tab","The Recommendations blade","The Regulatory compliance dashboard","The Inventory blade"],0,"The alert's Take action tab has mitigation guidance, related recommendations, automated responses and suppression options. Recommendations cover posture, not a specific alert.","Respond to security incidents: Perform threat response with Microsoft Defender for Cloud",[null,"The Recommendations page covers posture findings across resources, not response actions for this specific alert.","The Regulatory compliance dashboard maps controls to standards; it has no per-alert mitigation or automation options.","Inventory lists resources and their coverage; it does not host alert response actions."],1],["sa55",0,2,"In Sentinel, the analyst wants to visually expand an incident's entities to find related alerts, hosts and accounts. Which tool fits?",["The data connector status page","The investigation graph","The workspace usage workbook","The analytics rule wizard"],1,"The investigation graph starts from an incident's entities and lets you expand related alerts and entities. A workbook shows prepared charts, not interactive entity pivoting.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",["The data connector page shows connector status and configuration, not relationships between incident entities.",null,"The usage workbook shows ingestion volume charts, not an interactive graph of incident entities.","The analytics rule wizard is for creating or editing detection rules, not investigating incidents."],1],["sa56",0,2,"An analyst wants to know whether an account's activity is unusual compared with its normal pattern and its peers. Where in Sentinel should they look?",["The Content hub solution page","The MITRE ATT&CK coverage page","The account's entity page with UEBA insights","The SOC optimization page"],2,"Entity pages show timelines, related alerts and UEBA anomalies with peer comparisons. MITRE coverage shows rule coverage, not user behavior.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",["Content hub solution pages describe installable content, not an account's behavior.","The MITRE ATT&CK page shows detection coverage by technique, not user behavior.",null,"SOC optimization recommends ingestion and coverage improvements; it has no per-account behavioral analysis."],2],["sa57",0,2,"An incident was caused by an approved penetration test. How should it be classified when resolved?",["False positive, because nothing malicious happened","True positive for malicious activity with no further notes","Expected activity (benign positive) with a security testing determination","Left active until the test report is published"],2,"The detection was correct but the activity was authorized, so it is an expected/benign-positive result. A false positive would suggest the rule is broken and push the team to tune a working detection.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["False positive means the detection logic was wrong, but here the rule correctly detected real attack-like activity.","True positive for malicious activity misrepresents an authorized test and skews metrics.",null,"Leaving the incident active clutters the queue; it can be resolved now with the correct classification and notes."],2],["sa58",0,2,"An analyst new to a complex incident wants a plain-language summary and recommended next steps inside the incident page. What should they use?",["The Sentinel workspace usage workbook","Microsoft Security Copilot embedded in the Defender portal","A search job on the SecurityIncident table","The Action center history"],1,"Embedded Security Copilot creates incident summaries and guided response suggestions in the incident view. A search job retrieves archived data; it doesn't summarize anything.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["The usage workbook shows ingestion metrics and has no incident summaries or guidance.",null,"A search job retrieves archived data into a table; it does not summarize an incident.","The Action center history lists remediation actions already taken, not a summary or next steps."],1],["sa59",0,2,"An alert contains a long, obfuscated PowerShell command. The analyst wants a quick explanation of what the script does. Which feature helps most?",["Security Copilot script analysis","Device discovery","Alert tuning rules for the PowerShell alert","Secure score"],0,"Copilot script analysis explains suspicious scripts and command lines in plain language. Alert tuning suppresses alerts; it doesn't explain them.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",[null,"Device discovery finds unmanaged devices on the network and says nothing about a script's behavior.","Alert tuning suppresses or resolves alerts; it does not explain the PowerShell.","Secure score measures posture; it cannot analyze a command line."],1],["sa60",0,2,"The analyst needs to see every process, file, registry and network event on a device in the minutes before and after an alert. Where should they look?",["The device's vulnerability list","The Secure score history","The Submissions page","The device timeline"],3,"The device timeline shows the device's events in time order, with filtering around the alert time. Vulnerability lists show missing patches, not activity.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["The vulnerability list shows missing patches and CVEs, not process, file or network activity.","Secure score history shows posture changes over time, not device events.","The Submissions page is for sending emails, files and URLs to Microsoft for analysis, not for viewing device activity.",null],2],["sa61",0,2,"An automated investigation is waiting for approval to quarantine a file. Where does the analyst approve it?",["The Advanced features page in endpoint settings","The Pending tab of the Action center","The Threat analytics report","The Secure score page"],1,"The Action center lists remediation actions awaiting approval (Pending) and completed actions (History). Advanced features settings turn capabilities on or off; they don't approve individual actions.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Advanced features settings turn capabilities on or off for the tenant; they do not list actions awaiting approval.",null,"Threat analytics reports describe threat campaigns and exposure; they have no approval queue.","Secure score tracks posture improvements and cannot approve remediation actions."],1],["sa62",0,2,"Defender for Identity reports suspected Golden Ticket usage. Which remediation invalidates forged Kerberos tickets?",["Reset the passwords of all users who logged on that day","Restart all domain controllers at the same time","Turn off Kerberos and use NTLM temporarily","Reset the KRBTGT password twice, waiting for replication between resets"],3,"Golden Tickets are signed with the KRBTGT hash. Resetting it twice (because the previous password is also accepted) invalidates the forged tickets. Resetting user passwords doesn't change the key that signs them.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Identity",["User password resets do not change the KRBTGT key that signs forged TGTs, so the Golden Tickets stay valid.","Restarting domain controllers does not change the KRBTGT key and would cause an outage for nothing.","Disabling Kerberos is not practical and does not invalidate the stolen KRBTGT secret.",null],3],["sa63",0,2,"Every analyst should follow the same containment checklist inside each ransomware incident in Sentinel. What provides this?",["Incident tasks added by an automation rule","A watchlist named 'checklist'","A bookmark on each incident","A workbook pinned to the SOC's shared dashboard"],0,"Automation rules can add incident tasks so every analyst sees and ticks off the same steps inside the incident. A workbook sits outside the incident and can't track completion.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",[null,"A watchlist named checklist is only queryable reference data; it does not appear as trackable steps inside incidents.","Bookmarks save hunting query results as evidence; they are not a checklist analysts work through.","A shared workbook sits outside the incident and cannot track which steps were completed for each one."],2],["sa64",0,2,"An analyst finds two Defender XDR incidents that are the same attack on the same user. What should they do?",["Keep both open so each gets separate metrics","Delete one incident from the queue","Link the alerts into one incident and resolve the other","Create a suppression rule for the second incident's alerts"],2,"Moving (linking) alerts into one incident keeps all the evidence together; the empty incident can then be resolved. Suppression rules would hide future real alerts.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Keeping duplicates splits evidence across two incidents and inflates metrics.","Incidents are not deleted from the queue; the right move is to consolidate the alerts and resolve the empty incident.",null,"Suppressing the second incident's alerts would hide future genuine alerts matching the same pattern."],2],["sa65",0,2,"After confirming a ransomware binary's SHA-256 hash, the SOC wants it blocked from running on every onboarded device. What should they create?",["An alert tuning rule for the hash","A Sentinel watchlist containing the hash","A device group for affected devices","A file hash indicator with the Block and remediate action"],3,"File indicators with Block and remediate prevent the file from running and remediate it wherever it is found. A watchlist can be used in queries but blocks nothing.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["Alert tuning suppresses alerts about the file but does not block it from running.","A watchlist can be referenced in queries but enforces nothing on endpoints.","A device group scopes permissions and automation; it does not block a file hash.",null],2],["sa66",0,2,"An impossible-travel alert shows sign-ins from London and Singapore ten minutes apart. The Singapore IP is the company's VPN egress. What is the best response?",["Confirm it's corporate egress, close as benign and tag the range as corporate","Disable the account and reset the password at once","Delete the anomaly detection policy","Add Singapore to a blocked countries list"],0,"Verifying the IP shows expected activity; tagging corporate ranges reduces future false alerts. Deleting the policy removes a useful detection.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"Disabling the account and resetting the password disrupts a legitimate user when the IP is known corporate VPN egress.","Deleting the anomaly policy removes a useful detection for everyone instead of tuning out known corporate IPs.","Blocking Singapore would block the company's own VPN egress and does not address the benign alert."],3],["sa67",0,2,"The analyst needs a list of SharePoint files a compromised account downloaded last week. Which data source gives this?",["Entra ID sign-in logs","Microsoft Purview Audit (the unified audit log)","The Defender for Endpoint timeline of the user's device","Azure Activity log"],1,"The unified audit log records SharePoint and OneDrive file actions such as FileDownloaded. Sign-in logs show authentication, not file access.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",["Sign-in logs record authentication to apps, not which SharePoint files were downloaded.",null,"The device timeline covers activity on that one device and misses downloads from other devices or browsers.","The Azure Activity log records Azure resource management operations, not SharePoint file access."],2],["sa68",0,2,"An analyst wants to run an enrichment playbook on demand against one Sentinel incident. How can they do this?",["Edit the analytics rule and rerun it","Create a new data connector for the playbook","Run the incident-triggered playbook from the incident's actions","Restore the incident's logs from the data lake"],2,"Playbooks with the incident trigger can be run manually from the incident page. Rerunning the rule doesn't trigger enrichment.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",["Rerunning the analytics rule creates new alerts at best and does not run the enrichment playbook.","Playbooks are not data connectors; creating a connector does nothing to run a Logic App.",null,"Restoring logs brings archived data back for queries; it does not trigger a playbook."],2],["sa69",0,2,"Defender for Cloud Apps shows a user mass-downloading files from SharePoint at 3 a.m. The analyst wants to block the account from signing in while the investigation continues. Which governance action fits?",["Dismiss the alert and watch for another one","Create a new file policy for future downloads","Suspend the user","Add the user to a Sentinel watchlist of VIP accounts"],2,"Suspending the user blocks sign-in (through Entra ID for Microsoft 365) straight away, which stops the activity. A new file policy only affects future matching files and does not stop this user.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Cloud Apps",["Dismissing the alert leaves the account active while it may be exfiltrating data.","A new file policy applies to matching files going forward and does not stop this user from signing in.",null,"A VIP watchlist entry changes how queries treat the user but does not block sign-in."],3],["sa70",0,2,"A device was isolated during an incident, then cleaned and confirmed safe. What should the analyst do so the user can work normally again?",["Release the device from isolation in the device page","Offboard the device and onboard it again","Run the investigation package collection once more","Turn off network protection on that device"],0,"Release from isolation restores normal network connectivity and is recorded in the Action center. Offboarding and re-onboarding is unnecessary and leaves a gap in monitoring.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",[null,"Offboarding and re-onboarding is unnecessary and creates a gap in monitoring.","Collecting another investigation package gathers data but leaves the device isolated.","Turning off network protection weakens the device and has nothing to do with lifting isolation."],1],["sa71",0,3,"Which KQL filter returns only events from the last 24 hours in a Defender advanced hunting table?",["| where Timestamp < ago(1d)","| where Timestamp > ago(1d)","| take 1d","| summarize by ago(1d)"],1,"ago(1d) is the time 24 hours ago, so Timestamp greater than it means newer events. Using < returns everything older than a day.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Timestamp < ago(1d) returns events older than 24 hours, the opposite of what is needed.",null,"take limits the number of rows returned and does not accept a time span such as 1d.","summarize by ago(1d) groups by a constant value and does not filter to the last day."],1],["sa72",0,3,"A hunter searches ProcessCommandLine for the whole word 'mimikatz' across billions of rows. Which operator is usually fastest?",["contains","matches regex","startswith_cs","has"],3,"has uses the term index to match whole terms, so it is much faster than contains, which scans for substrings. Use contains only for partial matches inside a word.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["contains scans for substrings and cannot use the term index, so it is slower on large tables.","Regular expressions are the most expensive string matching and should be a last resort.","startswith_cs only matches the beginning of the value, so it would miss mimikatz appearing later in the command line.",null],3],["sa73",0,3,"Which query counts failed sign-ins per user?",["SigninLogs | project count() by UserPrincipalName | where ResultType != \"0\"","SigninLogs | extend count() | where ResultType == \"0\"","SigninLogs | where ResultType != \"0\" | take UserPrincipalName","SigninLogs | where ResultType != \"0\" | summarize count() by UserPrincipalName"],3,"summarize count() by groups rows and counts each group; filtering first on non-zero ResultType keeps only failures. project and take don't aggregate.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["project does not aggregate, and count() by is not valid inside project; the filter is also applied after the attempted count.","extend cannot take count() as an aggregation, and ResultType == \"0\" selects successful sign-ins, not failures.","take returns a number of rows and does not accept a column name or produce counts per user.",null],2],["sa74",0,3,"A hunter wants logon counts in one-hour buckets to spot spikes. Which expression belongs in the summarize?",["count() by bin(Timestamp, 1h)","count() by Timestamp","count() by ago(1h)","count() by format_datetime(1h)"],0,"bin() rounds timestamps down into fixed-size buckets, so each hour becomes one row. Grouping by the raw Timestamp gives one row per unique timestamp.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",[null,"Grouping by the raw Timestamp gives one row per unique time value, not hourly buckets.","ago(1h) returns a single point in time, so grouping by it would not create hourly buckets.","format_datetime formats a datetime value as text and does not take 1h as its argument; it does not create buckets."],2],["sa75",0,3,"A hunter wants to add process details from DeviceProcessEvents to network connections in DeviceNetworkEvents, matching on DeviceId and process ID. Which operator does this?",["union","join","extend","distinct"],1,"join combines columns from two tables on matching keys. union stacks rows from tables without matching them.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["union stacks rows from multiple tables without matching them on keys, so columns would not be combined.",null,"extend adds calculated columns from the same row; it cannot bring in columns from another table.","distinct removes duplicate rows from a single result set and does not combine tables."],2],["sa76",0,3,"A hunter has 20 known-bad IP addresses from a threat report and wants to reuse them in several parts of a query. What is the cleanest approach?",["Repeat twenty separate or conditions in each where clause of the query","Use project badIPs at the start of the query","let badIPs = dynamic([...]); then where RemoteIP in (badIPs)","Put the list in a comment above the query"],2,"let defines a named value once; in() checks membership against the dynamic array. Repeating conditions works but is error-prone and hard to maintain.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Twenty repeated or conditions work but are error-prone and must be edited everywhere when the list changes.","project selects columns from a table; it cannot define a reusable list of values.",null,"A comment is ignored by the query engine, so the IPs would not be used for filtering."],2],["sa77",0,3,"What is the difference between project and extend in KQL?",["project adds rows; extend adds tables","project picks the output columns; extend adds new columns to the existing ones","project sorts results; extend filters them","They are identical aliases for the same operator"],1,"extend keeps all existing columns and adds calculated ones, while project returns only the columns you list (and can calculate them too).","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Neither operator adds rows or tables; both work on the columns of the current result.",null,"Sorting is done with order by or sort by, and filtering with where, not project or extend.","They behave differently: project drops columns you do not list, while extend keeps them all."],1],["sa78",0,3,"Which advanced hunting table holds delivery details such as sender, recipient, subject and delivery action for each email?",["EmailUrlInfo","DeviceEvents","IdentityLogonEvents","EmailEvents"],3,"EmailEvents has one row per email with sender, recipient, subject and delivery details. EmailUrlInfo lists only the URLs found in emails.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["EmailUrlInfo lists URLs found in emails, not the sender, recipient, subject and delivery action.","DeviceEvents holds miscellaneous endpoint events such as ASR and exploit protection, not email data.","IdentityLogonEvents records authentication activity from identity sources, not email delivery.",null],1],["sa79",0,3,"A hunter looks for encoded PowerShell launched by Office apps on endpoints. Which table should the query start from?",["DeviceProcessEvents","DeviceNetworkEvents","EmailAttachmentInfo","CloudAppEvents"],0,"DeviceProcessEvents records process creation with command lines and parent processes (InitiatingProcessFileName). Network events have connections but not full process-creation detail.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",[null,"DeviceNetworkEvents records network connections, not process creation with full command lines.","EmailAttachmentInfo describes email attachments, not processes running on endpoints.","CloudAppEvents holds activity in cloud apps and services, not endpoint process launches."],2],["sa80",0,3,"Which advanced hunting table has on-premises Active Directory authentication events seen by Defender for Identity?",["DeviceLogonEvents","AADSignInEventsBeta","IdentityLogonEvents","EmailEvents"],2,"IdentityLogonEvents records authentication seen by Defender for Identity on domain controllers (and some cloud logons). DeviceLogonEvents comes from endpoints, not DC traffic.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["DeviceLogonEvents comes from Defender for Endpoint on devices, not authentication traffic seen on domain controllers.","AADSignInEventsBeta holds Entra ID (cloud) sign-ins, not on-premises Active Directory authentication.",null,"EmailEvents contains email delivery details and has nothing to do with AD authentication."],1],["sa81",0,3,"During a Sentinel hunt, an analyst finds suspicious rows and wants to save them with notes and entities so they can later be added to an incident. What should they create?",["A watchlist","A workbook","A summary rule","A bookmark"],3,"Bookmarks keep query results with notes, tags and entity mappings, and can be attached to or used to create incidents. A watchlist holds reference data, not evidence.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["A watchlist holds reference data such as VIP lists, not evidence with notes from a hunt.","A workbook visualizes query results but does not save specific rows with notes and entities for incidents.","A summary rule writes scheduled aggregations to a table; it does not save specific findings as evidence.",null],2],["sa82",0,3,"A hunter wants to be notified as soon as new events match a hunting query during an active investigation, without creating an analytics rule. Which feature fits?",["Livestream","A search job","A restore operation","Content hub"],0,"Livestream runs a hunting query continuously against new data and notifies you of matches. Search jobs look back at stored data rather than watching new events.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",[null,"A search job scans stored long-term data asynchronously; it does not watch new events as they arrive.","Restore brings archived data back for querying and does not notify you of new matches.","Content hub installs solutions and content; it does not run queries against new data."],2],["sa83",0,3,"A new IOC must be checked against firewall logs from 18 months ago that sit only in long-term retention. Which feature is designed for this?",["Livestream","A search job","An NRT analytics rule","A workbook"],1,"Search jobs scan long-term data asynchronously and write matches to a new table you can query. Livestream and NRT rules only look at newly arriving data.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["Livestream only watches newly arriving data, so it cannot see logs from 18 months ago.",null,"NRT rules run every minute on newly ingested data and cannot scan long-term retention.","Workbooks run interactive queries on the analytics tier and are not designed to scan long-term retention."],3],["sa84",0,3,"A hunter wants a daily KQL query over data lake data whose results are written to an analytics-tier table for detections to use. What should they create?",["A livestream session on the data lake tables","An automation rule","A KQL job in the Sentinel data lake","A watchlist"],2,"Data lake KQL jobs run queries (once or on a schedule) against the lake and can promote results to the analytics tier. Automation rules act on incidents, not on data.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["Livestream watches new analytics-tier data interactively; it does not run scheduled jobs on data lake tables or write results to a table.","Automation rules act on incidents and alerts, not on scheduled queries over data.",null,"A watchlist is static reference data and cannot run a daily query."],3],["sa85",0,3,"The team wants to use Python, MSTICPy and machine learning to analyze Sentinel data beyond what KQL alone can do. What should they use?",["Workbooks with KQL visualizations","Watchlists","Sentinel notebooks (Jupyter)","Automation rules"],2,"Notebooks run Jupyter with libraries such as MSTICPy for enrichment, visualization and ML over Sentinel data. Workbooks only show KQL results.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["Workbooks visualize KQL results but cannot run Python, MSTICPy or machine learning code.","Watchlists are reference data lists and run no analysis at all.",null,"Automation rules handle incidents (assign, tag, run playbooks), not data science analysis."],1],["sa86",0,3,"The SOC wants analyst reports on an active ransomware campaign, with which of their own devices are exposed and which mitigations are missing. Where is this?",["Threat analytics in Defender XDR","The MITRE ATT&CK coverage page in Sentinel","The Content hub","The Action center"],0,"Threat analytics reports combine Microsoft threat research with your tenant's exposure, alerts and mitigation status. MITRE coverage shows only which techniques your rules cover.","Perform threat hunting: Analyze threat intelligence",[null,"The MITRE ATT&CK page shows which techniques your rules cover, not campaign reports with your exposure and missing mitigations.","Content hub installs solutions and detections; it does not publish campaign analysis tied to your devices.","The Action center lists remediation actions, not threat reports or exposure details."],2],["sa87",0,3,"A hunter wants one query that finds connections to a malicious IP across logs from three firewall vendors without writing three separate queries. What should they use?",["A union of three raw tables with different column names","A separate workspace for each vendor","Three near-real-time rules, one per vendor","ASIM parsers such as _Im_NetworkSession"],3,"ASIM normalizes different sources into shared schemas, so one parser query covers every vendor. A raw union still needs field mapping for each vendor.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["A raw union still needs vendor-specific field names for each table, so the query is effectively three queries glued together.","Separate workspaces make cross-vendor querying harder, not easier.","Three NRT rules are three separate detections, not one hunting query across vendors.",null],2],["sa88",0,3,"An analyst who is new to KQL wants to describe a hunt in plain English and get a starting advanced hunting query. What can help?",["The Action center query builder","Security Copilot generating KQL in advanced hunting","The Secure score query tool","A Defender for Cloud recommendation"],1,"Security Copilot in advanced hunting can turn natural-language requests into KQL, which the analyst should review before running. The other options don't write queries.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["The Action center lists remediation actions and has no query builder.",null,"Secure score measures posture and has no tool that writes hunting queries.","Defender for Cloud recommendations are posture fixes, not KQL queries for hunting."],1],["sa89",0,3,"A hunter wants to see what a compromised service account could reach (its blast radius) by following relationships between identities, devices and resources. Which capability fits?",["A take 100 query on the SigninLogs table for the account","A hunting graph built on the Sentinel graph","A watchlist of service accounts","An alert tuning rule for the account"],1,"Graph-based hunting models relationships between entities, so you can explore paths and blast radius from a starting entity. A flat query of sign-ins doesn't show what the account can reach.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["A flat query of the account's sign-ins shows where it logged in, not what resources its relationships let it reach.",null,"A watchlist of service accounts is only a reference list and shows no relationships or reach.","An alert tuning rule suppresses alerts and says nothing about what the account can access."],2],["sa90",0,3,"The AdditionalFields column holds JSON, and the hunter needs one property from it as a column. Which KQL approach works?",["project AdditionalFields.PropName as text()","where AdditionalFields has_json PropName","extend Prop = tostring(parse_json(AdditionalFields).PropName)","summarize by json(AdditionalFields)"],2,"parse_json turns the string into a dynamic object, you read the property with dot notation, and tostring makes it a usable string column. The other forms are not valid KQL.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["This is not valid KQL: dynamic properties need parse_json first, and there is no text() function used this way.","has_json is not a KQL operator, and a where clause filters rows instead of extracting a property as a column.",null,"json() is not a KQL function, and summarize by would group rows rather than extract one property."],2],["sa91",0,3,"A hunter wants to flag days when a user's outbound data volume is statistically unusual compared with that user's own history. Which KQL approach fits?",["make-series then series_decompose_anomalies()","distinct then take 10","sort by bytes then take the top 10 rows for each user","union then project-away"],0,"make-series builds a time series for each entity and series_decompose_anomalies scores outliers against the trend and seasonality. Sorting or taking the top values ignores each user's baseline.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",[null,"distinct and take just list a few unique values and do not compare anything with a user's history.","Sorting by bytes and taking top rows finds the largest values overall, not days that are unusual for that user's own baseline.","union combines tables and project-away drops columns; neither detects statistical anomalies."],3],["sa92",0,3,"A hunting query has found real attacker activity twice. What should the team do so they are alerted automatically next time?",["Bookmark the results and rerun the same query by hand when needed","Add the query text to a watchlist","Export the results to a CSV file each week","Save the query as a custom detection or analytics rule"],3,"Turning a proven hunting query into a detection rule runs it on a schedule and raises alerts. Bookmarks save past findings but won't catch new activity.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Bookmarks preserve past findings, and rerunning a query by hand depends on someone remembering to do it.","Watchlists store reference data; placing query text there never runs it.","Weekly CSV exports are manual reports of past results and raise no alerts on new activity.",null],2],["x1",0,1,"The endpoint team wants users to see a notification when the attack surface reduction rule that stops Office apps from creating child processes fires, but still let a user unblock the action for a real business need. Which rule mode fits?",["Audit mode","Warn mode","Block mode","Not configured, with a folder exclusion"],1,"Warn mode blocks the action but shows a dialog that lets the user unblock it, which gives protection with a business escape hatch. Audit mode only logs events and never interrupts or informs the user.","Domain 1: Attack surface reduction rule modes",["Audit mode only records what the rule would have blocked; users see nothing and cannot choose to continue.",null,"Block mode stops the action with no option for the user to unblock it.","Leaving the rule unconfigured gives no protection at all, and an exclusion just skips the check."],2],["x2",0,1,"A Windows server's tags and name match the membership rules of two different Defender for Endpoint device groups. Which group will the device belong to?",["Both groups, with the stricter automation level applied","The group that was created first","Only the higher-ranked group whose rules it matches","Neither group until an administrator assigns it by hand"],2,"A device joins only the highest-ranked device group whose rules it matches, so rank order decides membership. Devices never belong to two groups at once, so 'both groups' cannot happen.","Domain 1: Device groups and ranking",["A device is never a member of two device groups at once, so settings are not combined.","Creation date does not matter; the group rank order decides membership.",null,"Rule-based groups assign devices automatically; no manual assignment is needed."],2],["x3",0,1,"A company wants to manage permissions for Defender for Endpoint, Defender for Identity and Defender for Office 365 from one place in the Defender portal using custom roles. What should they activate?",["Microsoft Defender XDR Unified role-based access control","Azure RBAC role assignments on the Sentinel resource group","The Microsoft Entra Global Reader role for every analyst","Separate device groups for each workload"],0,"Defender XDR Unified RBAC lets admins build custom roles covering several Defender workloads from one permissions page. Azure RBAC on a resource group controls Azure resources such as Sentinel, not the Defender workloads' portal permissions.","Domain 1: Defender XDR portal RBAC",[null,"Azure RBAC governs Azure resources like the Sentinel workspace, not permissions inside Defender for Endpoint, Identity and Office 365.","Global Reader is a broad read-only directory role and cannot express custom Defender permissions.","Device groups scope Defender for Endpoint devices; they do not cover Identity or Office 365 permissions."],2],["x4",0,1,"A company keeps a third-party antivirus as its primary product, so Microsoft Defender Antivirus runs in passive mode. They want Defender for Endpoint to remediate malicious artifacts its EDR sensor detects anyway. What should be turned on?",["Tamper protection","Controlled folder access in audit mode","Network protection in block mode","EDR in block mode"],3,"EDR in block mode lets Defender for Endpoint block and remediate artifacts found by EDR even when Defender Antivirus is passive. Tamper protection only stops security settings from being changed; it does not add remediation.","Domain 1: Defender for Endpoint configuration",["Tamper protection stops settings being changed; it does not make EDR findings get remediated.","Controlled folder access guards folders, and in audit mode it only logs events.","Network protection blocks connections to bad destinations; it does not remediate artifacts that EDR found.",null],3],["x5",0,1,"The SOC wants to stop untrusted applications from changing files in users' Documents and Desktop folders, a common ransomware behavior, while trusted apps keep working. Which Defender for Endpoint feature fits best?",["Controlled folder access","The ASR rule 'Use advanced protection against ransomware'","A file hash indicator with a Block action","Device control removable storage policies"],0,"Controlled folder access protects chosen folders so only allowed or trusted apps can modify files in them. The ASR ransomware rule inspects executables for ransomware traits but does not guard specific folders from writes by untrusted apps.","Domain 1: Controlled folder access",[null,"This ASR rule looks for ransomware traits in executables; it does not protect chosen folders from untrusted writers.","A hash indicator blocks one known file, not every unknown app that tries to encrypt documents.","Device control governs USB and removable storage, not which apps may write to local folders."],3],["x6",0,1,"A web app lets customers upload documents into Azure Blob Storage. The security team wants each new blob checked for malware soon after upload. What should they enable?",["Defender for Servers Plan 2 on the web app's VMs","Foundational CSPM for the subscription","Defender for Storage with on-upload malware scanning","Defender for App Service on the web app that performs the uploads"],2,"Defender for Storage's malware scanning inspects blobs when they are uploaded and reports results. Defender for Servers protects the VM operating systems, not objects stored in a storage account.","Domain 1: Defender for Cloud workload protection",["Defender for Servers protects the VM operating systems, not objects stored in Blob Storage.","Foundational CSPM gives posture recommendations and secure score, not malware scanning of blobs.",null,"Defender for App Service watches the App Service platform for attacks; it does not scan stored blobs."],2],["x7",0,1,"The cloud security team wants to see how an internet-facing VM with a known vulnerability could lead an attacker to a storage account holding sensitive data. Which Defender for Cloud option provides this attack path analysis?",["Foundational CSPM, which is on by default","The Defender CSPM plan","Defender for Resource Manager","Regulatory compliance dashboard"],1,"Attack path analysis and the cloud security explorer are part of the paid Defender CSPM plan. Foundational CSPM gives secure score and basic recommendations but not attack path analysis, which makes it the tempting but wrong choice.","Domain 1: Defender for Cloud CSPM",["Foundational CSPM is free and gives recommendations and secure score, but not attack path analysis.",null,"Defender for Resource Manager watches Azure management operations; it does not map attack paths.","The regulatory compliance dashboard maps controls to standards; it does not trace attack paths."],3],["x8",0,1,"Administrators want management ports on Azure VMs closed by default and opened only on approved request for a limited time. Which Defender for Cloud plan includes just-in-time VM access?",["Defender for Servers Plan 1","Foundational CSPM","Defender for Resource Manager","Defender for Servers Plan 2"],3,"Just-in-time VM access is a Defender for Servers Plan 2 feature that locks down management ports and opens them on request. Plan 1 focuses on the Defender for Endpoint integration and does not include JIT.","Domain 1: Defender for Servers plans",["Plan 1 centers on the Defender for Endpoint integration and does not include just-in-time VM access.","Foundational CSPM gives posture recommendations only, not JIT port control.","Defender for Resource Manager monitors management operations; it does not lock down VM ports.",null],1],["x9",0,1,"The identity team wants Defender for Identity to spot suspicious certificate requests and other attacks aimed at the company's enterprise certificate authority. What should they do?",["Install a Defender for Identity sensor on the AD CS servers","Rely on the sensors already on the domain controllers","Onboard the CA servers to Defender for Endpoint only","Forward the CA servers' Security logs to Sentinel through CEF"],0,"Defender for Identity supports sensors on AD CS servers so it can see certificate services activity directly. Domain controller sensors do not see what happens inside the certificate authority service, so they are not enough.","Domain 1: Defender for Identity sensors",[null,"Domain controller sensors do not see activity inside the certificate services on the CA servers.","Defender for Endpoint protects the endpoint but does not provide Defender for Identity's AD CS detections.","Forwarding logs to Sentinel does not give Defender for Identity the data it needs for its own detections."],2],["x10",0,1,"Attackers keep sending zero-day malware in email attachments that signature scanning misses. Which Defender for Office 365 feature opens attachments in a sandbox before users can reach them?",["Anti-malware policy with common attachment filter","Safe Links","Safe Attachments","Zero-hour auto purge"],2,"Safe Attachments detonates attachments in a sandbox to find unknown malware before delivery. The anti-malware policy relies on signatures and file type filters, so it misses the new malware described.","Domain 1: Defender for Office 365 Safe Attachments",["Signature and file type filtering misses brand-new malware, which is the problem described.","Safe Links checks URLs at click time; it does not detonate attachments.",null,"Zero-hour auto purge acts after delivery when a verdict changes; it does not sandbox attachments first."],2],["x11",0,1,"An auditor needs to view Sentinel incidents, analytics rules and workbooks but must not change anything. Which built-in role should be assigned?",["Microsoft Sentinel Responder","Microsoft Sentinel Reader","Microsoft Sentinel Automation Contributor","Log Analytics Contributor"],1,"Microsoft Sentinel Reader gives read-only access to Sentinel data and resources. Responder can also assign and close incidents, which is more than an auditor needs.","Domain 1: Sentinel roles",["Responder can assign and close incidents, which is more access than a read-only auditor should have.",null,"Automation Contributor is meant for the Sentinel service to run playbooks, not for people viewing data.","Log Analytics Contributor can change workspace settings, far beyond read-only access."],1],["x12",0,1,"Linux servers must send only authentication messages at warning level and above to Sentinel. What is the recommended way to collect them?",["Install the legacy Log Analytics agent on each server and enable every syslog facility","Send the logs to the Logs Ingestion API from a cron job","Point syslog at a storage account and import it weekly","Use the Azure Monitor Agent with a DCR selecting the auth facilities and minimum level"],3,"The Azure Monitor Agent with a data collection rule can filter syslog by facility and minimum severity, keeping only what is needed. The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired, and enabling every facility would raise cost.","Domain 1: Syslog through AMA and DCRs",["The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired, and collecting every facility raises cost for data nobody needs.","The Logs Ingestion API is for custom apps; building cron jobs for syslog is fragile and needless.","A weekly import is not timely enough for detection and adds manual work.",null],2],["x13",0,1,"A connector brings in a table where one large column is never used, and some rows are debug noise. The SOC wants to drop both before the data is stored. What should they use?",["A workspace summary rule","A watchlist of rows to ignore","An ingestion-time transformation in the data collection rule","An automation rule that deletes the unwanted rows after they arrive"],2,"A DCR transformation runs KQL on incoming data, so it can remove columns and filter rows before they are stored and billed. A summary rule works on data that has already been ingested, so it cannot reduce what was stored.","Domain 1: Data collection rule transformations",["Summary rules aggregate data after ingestion, so the unwanted data is still stored and billed.","Watchlists are reference lists for queries; they cannot filter data before it is stored.",null,"Automation rules act on incidents, not on log rows, and deleting after arrival does not avoid ingestion cost."],2],["x14",0,1,"A scheduled rule runs every 5 minutes and looks back 5 minutes. Some events arrive late because of ingestion delay and are never evaluated. What is the recommended fix?",["Enable alert grouping so that late events are added to the earlier alert from the previous run","Lengthen the lookback beyond the run interval and filter on ingestion_time() to avoid duplicates","Raise the rule's alert threshold so fewer events are needed","Shorten the lookback to 1 minute so each run finishes faster"],1,"Making the lookback longer than the frequency catches late rows, and filtering on ingestion_time() stops the same rows being alerted twice. Alert grouping only groups alerts that were created; it cannot evaluate events the query never saw.","Domain 1: Scheduled analytics rules and ingestion delay",["Alert grouping only combines alerts that were created; events the query never saw still produce nothing.",null,"Raising the threshold makes the rule less sensitive and does nothing about late-arriving rows.","A shorter lookback misses even more late events, making the gap worse."],3],["x15",0,1,"The SOC turned on a built-in anomaly analytics rule but sees no incidents from it. Where do its results appear?",["As high-severity incidents in the Defender portal incident queue","In the SecurityIncident table only","In a watchlist named after the rule","In the Anomalies table, for use in hunting and other rules"],3,"Anomaly rules write records to the Anomalies table instead of creating alerts or incidents, so analysts use them in hunting or in other detections. They do not create incidents, which is why none appear in the queue.","Domain 1: Anomaly analytics rules",["Anomaly rules do not create incidents, so nothing reaches the incident queue from them directly.","SecurityIncident stores incidents, and anomaly rules do not create any.","Watchlists are reference lists that people upload; rules do not write results into them.",null],2],["x16",0,1,"One automation rule raises incident severity for a certain rule's incidents. A second automation rule assigns high-severity incidents to Tier 2. Both trigger on incident creation, but assignments are being missed. What should be fixed?",["Set the rules' order so the severity rule runs before the assignment rule","Merge both automation rules into one Logic Apps playbook triggered by the incident","Change the second rule to trigger on alert creation","Give the automation rules the Sentinel Contributor role"],0,"Automation rules run in order of their order number, and a later rule sees changes made by earlier ones, so the severity rule must run first. Moving the logic into a playbook would work but is heavier and does not fix the ordering cause.","Domain 1: Automation rule order",[null,"A playbook could do both steps but is heavier to build and run; the real cause is rule order.","Alert-created triggers run on alerts, not on the incident whose severity the first rule changed.","The problem is ordering, not permissions; automation rules already act on incidents without that role."],3],["x17",0,1,"An analyst looking at an IP address inside a Sentinel investigation wants to run a playbook that looks up only that IP. Which playbook trigger should the playbook use?",["Microsoft Sentinel incident trigger","Microsoft Sentinel alert trigger","Microsoft Sentinel entity trigger","A recurrence trigger every 5 minutes"],2,"The entity trigger lets an analyst run a playbook on demand against a single entity such as an IP. An incident trigger passes the whole incident, not one chosen entity.","Domain 1: Playbook triggers",["The incident trigger passes the whole incident, not a single entity the analyst picks.","The alert trigger passes an alert, not one chosen entity from an investigation.",null,"A recurrence trigger runs on a schedule, not on demand against one IP."],2],["x18",0,1,"A playbook uses its managed identity to add comments and change status on Sentinel incidents. Which role should the managed identity get on the workspace?",["Microsoft Sentinel Reader","Microsoft Sentinel Automation Contributor","Logic App Contributor","Microsoft Sentinel Responder"],3,"Responder can update incidents, including comments and status, which is exactly what the playbook needs. Automation Contributor is the role granted to the Sentinel service so automation rules can run playbooks; it does not grant incident updates to the playbook's identity.","Domain 1: Playbook permissions",["Reader cannot add comments or change status on incidents.","Automation Contributor is given to the Sentinel service so automation rules can run playbooks, not to update incidents.","Logic App Contributor manages the Logic App itself; it gives no rights over Sentinel incidents.",null],3],["x19",0,1,"A Defender XDR custom detection rule finds devices running a known credential dumping command. The SOC wants each matching device isolated automatically. What should they configure?",["A Sentinel automation rule that closes the incident","The rule's automated action to isolate the device","An alert tuning rule for the detection","A device group with a Full automation level"],1,"Custom detection rules can take response actions on the entities they find, such as isolating the device. A Full automation level governs automated investigation remediation, not isolation from a custom detection.","Domain 1: Custom detection rule actions",["Closing an incident in Sentinel does not isolate any device.",null,"Alert tuning rules hide or resolve alerts; they never take response actions on devices.","Automation levels govern automated investigation remediation, not isolation triggered by a custom detection."],2],["x20",0,1,"An analytics rule must exclude accounts listed in a watchlist whose alias is ServiceAccounts. Which KQL function reads that watchlist?",["_GetWatchlist('ServiceAccounts')","externaldata('ServiceAccounts')","lookup('ServiceAccounts')","materialize('ServiceAccounts')"],0,"_GetWatchlist() with the alias returns the watchlist rows so a rule can join or filter against them. externaldata reads files from external storage URLs and does not know about Sentinel watchlists.","Domain 1: Watchlists",[null,"externaldata reads files from storage URLs; it has no link to Sentinel watchlists.","lookup joins against a table expression; it cannot fetch a watchlist by its alias on its own.","materialize caches a subquery result within a query; it does not read watchlists."],1],["x21",0,1,"A company runs Windows servers in its own datacenter and wants them covered by Defender for Servers, including the Defender for Endpoint integration, managed from Defender for Cloud. What is the recommended approach?",["Move the servers into Azure before turning on any plan","Onboard the servers only with a local script and skip Defender for Cloud","Connect the servers with Azure Arc and enable Defender for Servers","Install the Defender for Identity sensor on each server"],2,"Azure Arc makes on-premises servers Azure resources, so Defender for Servers can cover them and deploy the Defender for Endpoint integration. A local onboarding script gives endpoint protection but not the Defender for Cloud plan coverage asked for.","Domain 1: Defender for Cloud and hybrid servers",["Migrating servers to Azure is not required; Azure Arc covers servers where they are.","A local script gives endpoint protection but not the Defender for Servers coverage managed in Defender for Cloud.",null,"The Defender for Identity sensor belongs on identity servers like domain controllers, not on every server."],2],["x22",0,1,"The company uses non-persistent VDI desktops that are recreated at each logon. After onboarding them with the normal local script, the device inventory fills with duplicate entries. What should be used instead?",["The VDI onboarding script for non-persistent devices","Tamper protection in the golden image","A separate Defender for Endpoint tenant for VDI","Device discovery in standard mode on the VDI host pool"],0,"Microsoft provides VDI onboarding scripts that handle non-persistent machines so each desktop keeps a consistent identity. Tamper protection guards settings and does nothing about duplicate device records.","Domain 1: Onboarding VDI devices",[null,"Tamper protection guards security settings and does nothing about duplicate device records.","Using a separate tenant splits visibility and still does not handle non-persistent identities.","Device discovery finds unmanaged devices; it does not change how VDI desktops are onboarded."],2],["x23",0,1,"EU logs must stay in an EU region and US logs in a US region, but one SOC team investigates both. Which Sentinel workspace design fits best?",["One workspace in the US that ingests all data","A workspace in each region with multi-workspace views for the SOC","One workspace in the EU with a data export rule copying US data into it","No workspace, with data read only through advanced hunting"],1,"Separate regional workspaces keep data where it must reside, and multi-workspace incident and hunting views let one SOC work across them. A single US workspace would store EU data outside the EU and break the residency requirement.","Domain 1: Sentinel workspace design",["Ingesting EU logs into a US workspace stores them outside the EU and breaks the residency rule.",null,"Copying US data into the EU workspace breaks the US residency requirement in the other direction.","Advanced hunting does not replace a Sentinel workspace for third-party logs, retention and analytics rules."],3],["x24",0,1,"Application owners should query logs from their own VMs in the shared Log Analytics workspace without seeing other teams' data or Sentinel incidents. What should be used?",["Assign each owner Log Analytics Reader on the whole workspace so they can filter","Create a separate Sentinel instance for each team","Give each owner Sentinel Reader on the workspace","Resource-context access based on the owners' permissions on their VMs"],3,"With resource-context access, users with read rights on their own resources see only log data tied to those resources. Workspace-wide Log Analytics Reader would expose every team's data.","Domain 1: Workspace access control",["Workspace-wide Log Analytics Reader shows every team's logs, which is what must be avoided.","Separate Sentinel instances per team add cost and complexity when resource-context access solves it.","Sentinel Reader exposes incidents and all Sentinel data in the workspace, not just the owners' own VMs.",null],3],["x25",0,1,"A small team wants Microsoft's recommended Safe Links, Safe Attachments and anti-phishing settings applied to all users quickly, with settings Microsoft keeps updated. What should they use?",["Custom policies copied by hand from another tenant","A mail flow rule for each protection","Standard or Strict preset security policies","Alert policies in the Defender portal"],2,"Preset security policies apply Microsoft's recommended protection settings as a bundle and are updated by Microsoft. Custom policies can match them but must be built and maintained by hand, which is not quick.","Domain 1: Defender for Office 365 preset policies",["Copying custom policies works but is slow, manual and not kept updated by Microsoft.","Mail flow rules cannot provide Safe Links or Safe Attachments protection.",null,"Alert policies raise notifications; they do not set protection settings."],1],["x26",0,2,"During live response, an analyst wants to run a custom PowerShell remediation script on a device with the run command. Where must the script be first?",["In the analyst's local Downloads folder","Attached as a comment on the incident","In the device's investigation package","In the live response library"],3,"Live response's run command executes scripts that have been uploaded to the live response library. The investigation package is collected from the device for analysis; it is not a place to stage scripts.","Domain 2: Live response",["Live response cannot run files straight from the analyst's own computer; they must be uploaded to the library.","Incident comments are for notes, not for staging scripts for live response.","The investigation package is data collected from the device, not a place to put scripts.",null],2],["x27",0,2,"A signed line-of-business tool was quarantined on 200 devices as a false positive. After restoring it, the SOC wants to stop it being flagged again with the narrowest reliable change. What should they do?",["Add an antivirus exclusion for the whole Program Files folder","Create an Allow indicator for the file's hash","Turn off real-time protection on the affected device group","Set the device group's automation level to None"],1,"An Allow indicator for the specific file hash tells Defender for Endpoint to trust that exact file. A folder exclusion also stops detections but is far broader, letting any malware in that folder go unscanned.","Domain 2: File indicators",["A whole-folder exclusion hides any malware placed in that folder, which is far too broad.",null,"Turning off real-time protection removes protection for the whole device group.","Automation level only changes automated remediation; the file would still be detected and alerted on."],3],["x28",0,2,"Automated investigation quarantined a file on many devices, and the SOC now confirms it was clean. Where can they reverse the remediation?",["The Action center history, using undo","The device timeline of one affected device","Threat analytics","The Submissions page"],0,"The Action center history lists completed remediation actions and lets analysts undo them. The device timeline shows events on one device but is not where remediation actions are reversed.","Domain 2: Action center",[null,"The device timeline shows events on one device; remediation actions are undone from the Action center.","Threat analytics holds reports on threats and campaigns; it does not manage remediation actions.","Submissions sends samples to Microsoft for review; it does not reverse quarantine actions already taken."],2],["x29",0,2,"A phishing campaign's URL has been confirmed malicious. The analyst needs to know which recipients actually clicked it. Which Defender for Office 365 tool is designed for this?",["The Safe Links policy settings page","Attack simulation training campaign reports","Threat Explorer, reviewing URL clicks","The quarantine review page"],2,"Threat Explorer shows email and URL click data, so the analyst can see who clicked the malicious link. Attack simulation reports only cover simulated phishing, not a real campaign.","Domain 2: Threat Explorer",["Safe Links settings define protection; they do not list who clicked a real campaign's URL.","Attack simulation reports cover simulated phishing only, not a real campaign.",null,"Quarantine holds messages that were blocked; delivered messages and their clicks are not there."],2],["x30",0,2,"A legitimate partner newsletter keeps being sent to junk by Defender for Office 365. The admin wants Microsoft to review the verdict. What should they do?",["Submit it on the Submissions page as should not have been blocked","Create a Safe Links policy for the partner's domain","Hard delete the messages in Threat Explorer","Report it as phishing from the user's mailbox"],0,"The Submissions page sends a false positive to Microsoft for analysis and can add an allow entry. Reporting it as phishing tells Microsoft the opposite of the truth and would reinforce the block.","Domain 2: Submissions",[null,"Safe Links rewrites and checks URLs; it does not change a spam verdict for the newsletter.","Hard deleting the messages removes them for good, the opposite of what the admin wants.","Reporting it as phishing tells Microsoft the message is bad and reinforces the block."],2],["x31",0,2,"A message was delivered to inboxes, and an hour later Microsoft's verdict changed to malware. Which feature can move the delivered copies out of the inboxes automatically?",["Safe Attachments dynamic delivery","Zero-hour auto purge","Anti-spam outbound policy","Tenant allow/block list"],1,"Zero-hour auto purge acts on messages already delivered when their verdict later changes to spam, phishing or malware. Dynamic delivery holds attachments during scanning before delivery, not after.","Domain 2: Zero-hour auto purge",["Dynamic delivery works during scanning before delivery, not after a later verdict change.",null,"Outbound spam policies govern mail sent out of the tenant, not delivered inbound messages.","The tenant allow/block list affects future mail flow; it does not move already delivered copies."],1],["x32",0,2,"Defender for Identity raises 'Suspected identity theft (pass-the-hash)' for an account. What does this alert indicate?",["The account's password was guessed after many failed logons from a single workstation","A forged Kerberos TGT was presented","The account's NTLM hash was used from a computer it is not normally used on","A service ticket was requested for an account with an SPN"],2,"Pass-the-hash means an attacker reused a stolen NTLM hash to authenticate, which Defender for Identity spots as the hash being used from an unusual computer. A forged TGT is a Golden Ticket, a different attack.","Domain 2: Defender for Identity alerts",["Password guessing shows up as brute-force alerts, not pass-the-hash.","A forged Kerberos TGT is a Golden Ticket attack, which has its own alert.",null,"Requesting service tickets for SPN accounts is Kerberoasting, a different alert."],1],["x33",0,2,"The SOC creates a dormant decoy account that nobody should ever use and wants an alert whenever anything authenticates with it. What should they do in Defender for Identity?",["Tag the account as a honeytoken","Add the account to the sensitive group list","Disable the account in Active Directory","Exclude the account from all detections"],0,"Tagging an account as a honeytoken makes Defender for Identity alert on any activity by it. Marking it sensitive affects lateral movement path reporting and alert context, but does not alert on every use.","Domain 2: Defender for Identity honeytokens",[null,"Sensitive accounts get extra attention in lateral movement paths, but ordinary use does not raise an alert.","Disabling the account may stop the attacker's logon, but Defender for Identity will not alert on attempts as a honeytoken would.","Excluding the account from detections is the opposite of wanting alerts on it."],2],["x34",0,2,"Entra ID Protection flagged one sign-in as risky. The analyst checked with the user and confirmed the user really did sign in from a hotel while traveling. What should the analyst do?",["Confirm the user compromised","Block the user until they return","Reset the user's MFA methods","Confirm the sign-in safe"],3,"Confirming the sign-in safe marks that specific detection as a false positive and feeds the risk model. Confirming the user compromised would push the user to high risk, the opposite of what the facts show.","Domain 2: Entra ID Protection",["Confirming the user compromised raises user risk to high, which is wrong for a legitimate sign-in.","Blocking the user punishes a real user for a sign-in that was confirmed legitimate.","Resetting MFA methods is a response to compromise, which did not happen here.",null],2],["x35",0,2,"An attacker stole a user's password and signed in. The password was reset, but the attacker's browser session keeps working. What should the analyst do next?",["Revoke the user's sessions and refresh tokens","Wait for the old password to expire","Delete and recreate the user account","Add the attacker's IP to a Sentinel watchlist"],0,"Revoking sessions invalidates refresh tokens and session cookies, forcing the attacker to sign in again with credentials they no longer have. A watchlist only helps detections; it does not end an active session.","Domain 2: Session revocation",[null,"Waiting does nothing; the attacker's existing session tokens can stay valid after a password change.","Deleting the account is destructive and unnecessary when sessions can be revoked.","A watchlist feeds detections but does not end the attacker's active session."],2],["x36",0,2,"During an investigation into data theft, the SOC wants to block downloads of labeled sensitive files from SharePoint on unmanaged devices in real time while users can still view them. What fits best?",["A Defender for Cloud Apps file policy with a daily scan","Suspend every user who downloaded files last week","A session policy using Conditional Access app control","A DLP alert policy that emails the SOC"],2,"A session policy through Conditional Access app control acts on activity in real time, such as blocking downloads on unmanaged devices. A file policy scans files at rest and cannot stop a download as it happens.","Domain 2: Defender for Cloud Apps session control",["A file policy scans stored files on a schedule and cannot stop a download as it happens.","Suspending many users is disruptive and does not target downloads on unmanaged devices.",null,"An alert policy only notifies the SOC; it does not block anything."],3],["x37",0,2,"A mailbox was accessed by an attacker. Legal needs to know which specific mail items the attacker opened or synced. What should the analyst search in Purview Audit?",["MailItemsAccessed records for that mailbox","Send records for the mailbox","Mailbox login records for the user only","Message trace results covering the last 10 days"],0,"MailItemsAccessed audit records show which mail items were read or synced and by which session, which is what legal needs. Message trace shows mail flow, not which messages were opened.","Domain 2: Purview Audit",[null,"Send records show messages sent from the mailbox, not items the attacker opened.","Mailbox login records show that someone signed in, not which items they read.","Message trace shows mail flow into and out of the tenant, not which messages were opened."],3],["x38",0,2,"A test VM that runs security tools raises the same Defender for Cloud alert every day. Analysts want these alerts dismissed automatically for that VM only. What should they create?",["A workflow automation that emails the owner","A Defender for Cloud alerts suppression rule scoped to the VM","An Azure Policy exemption for the subscription containing the VM","A Sentinel watchlist containing the VM name"],1,"An alerts suppression rule in Defender for Cloud dismisses matching alerts automatically, and it can be scoped to one resource. A policy exemption affects recommendations and compliance, not security alerts.","Domain 2: Defender for Cloud alerts",["Emailing the owner every day still leaves the alert open for analysts to handle.",null,"Policy exemptions affect recommendations and compliance, not Defender for Cloud security alerts.","A Sentinel watchlist does not change how Defender for Cloud raises or dismisses alerts."],2],["x39",0,2,"An analyst wants a suspicious executable detonated safely and a behavior report of its file, registry and network activity, starting from the file page in Defender for Endpoint. Which action should they use?",["Collect investigation package","Stop and quarantine file","Add an Allow indicator","Deep analysis"],3,"Deep analysis submits the file to a sandbox and returns a detailed behavior report. Stop and quarantine contains the file but produces no behavior analysis.","Domain 2: Defender for Endpoint file analysis",["The investigation package collects device data such as autoruns and logs; it does not detonate a file.","Stop and quarantine contains the file but produces no behavior report.","An Allow indicator trusts the file, the opposite of analyzing a suspicious one.",null],2],["x40",0,2,"Which Entra ID Protection risk type describes the probability that a given authentication request was not made by the identity's owner?",["User risk","Sign-in risk","Service principal risk","Device compliance risk"],1,"Sign-in risk scores a single authentication request. User risk is the tempting choice, but it measures the probability that the identity itself is compromised, across sign-ins.","Domain 2: Entra ID Protection risk types",["User risk measures the chance the identity itself is compromised, across sign-ins, not one request.",null,"Service principal risk applies to workload identities, not user authentication requests.","Device compliance is an Intune state, not an Entra ID Protection risk type."],1],["x41",0,2,"A device was isolated and the malicious process removed. The analyst now wants to check the whole disk for dormant malware copies. Which action fits?",["Quick antivirus scan","Restrict app execution","Full antivirus scan","Release from isolation"],2,"A full scan checks every file on the disk, which is needed to find dormant copies. A quick scan only checks common locations where malware usually runs, so it could miss them.","Domain 2: Defender for Endpoint response actions",["A quick scan checks common malware locations and can miss dormant copies elsewhere on disk.","Restrict app execution limits which apps can run; it does not look for malware files.",null,"Releasing from isolation lets the device talk to the network again before it is checked."],1],["x42",0,2,"For one device group, the SOC wants automated investigation remediation actions to happen without waiting for analyst approval. What should they set?",["The device group's automation level to Full","The device group's automation level to Semi for all folders","Tamper protection on the device group","An alert tuning rule for the device group"],0,"Full automation lets automated investigation remediate threats without approval. Semi levels still require approval for some or all remediation actions.","Domain 2: Automated investigation and response",[null,"Semi levels still require analyst approval for some or all remediation actions.","Tamper protection guards security settings; it has nothing to do with approval of remediation.","Alert tuning rules hide or resolve alerts; they do not change remediation approval."],2],["x43",0,2,"Attackers stole a vendor's code-signing certificate and are signing malware with it. The SOC wants to block anything signed with that certificate on all onboarded devices. What should they create?",["A file hash indicator for one known sample","A domain indicator for the vendor's website","An ASR rule in audit mode","A certificate indicator with a Block action"],3,"A certificate indicator blocks every file signed with that certificate, including samples not yet seen. A single file hash indicator only stops that one sample.","Domain 2: Certificate indicators",["A single hash indicator blocks one known sample, not new files signed with the same certificate.","Blocking the vendor's website does not stop signed malware already on devices.","Audit mode only logs; it blocks nothing.",null],3],["x44",0,2,"Defender for Identity alerts on suspected Kerberos SPN exposure: an attacker requested service tickets for several service accounts. Which remediation best reduces the impact?",["Disable Kerberos for the domain","Use gMSAs or long random passwords for the service accounts","Remove the service accounts from all their groups for the time being","Restart the domain controllers"],1,"Kerberoasting cracks service tickets offline, so strong, managed passwords such as gMSAs make cracking impractical. Removing group memberships may break services and does not stop cracking of the tickets already taken.","Domain 2: Kerberoasting response",["Disabling Kerberos would break authentication across the domain and push clients to weaker NTLM.",null,"Removing group memberships can break services and does not stop offline cracking of tickets already taken.","Restarting domain controllers does nothing to service tickets the attacker already holds."],3],["x45",0,2,"Legal requires that copies of a harmful email be removed so users cannot get them back from Recoverable Items. Which Threat Explorer action should be used?",["Move to junk folder","Soft delete","Hard delete","Move to deleted items"],2,"Hard delete purges messages so users cannot restore them. Soft delete moves them to a place users can still recover them from, which fails the legal requirement.","Domain 2: Email remediation",["Moving to junk leaves the message in the mailbox where users can still open it.","Soft delete leaves messages recoverable by users, which fails the legal requirement.",null,"Deleted Items can be opened and restored by the user."],3],["x46",0,2,"After several account takeovers, the SOC wants users with high user risk to be forced to change their password securely at next sign-in. What should be configured?",["A Conditional Access policy using user risk that requires a secure password change","A Defender for Cloud Apps anomaly policy","A Sentinel NRT rule that emails the users","An Entra group for risky users"],0,"A risk-based Conditional Access policy can require a secure password change when user risk is high, which also remediates the risk. A Sentinel rule could notify users but cannot enforce anything at sign-in.","Domain 2: Risk-based Conditional Access",[null,"Cloud Apps anomaly policies raise alerts; they do not force a password change at sign-in.","A Sentinel rule can email users but cannot enforce anything when they sign in.","A group alone does nothing unless a policy targets it, and risk changes too fast for manual groups."],3],["x47",0,2,"The SOC wants to disable a compromised on-premises Active Directory account directly from the Defender portal using Defender for Identity. What must be configured?",["A Defender for Endpoint device group for domain controllers","A Defender for Identity action account with the needed AD permissions","Password hash sync in Entra Connect","A Sentinel playbook that reads a domain admin password stored in Azure Key Vault"],1,"Defender for Identity uses an action account to perform remediation actions such as disabling users or resetting passwords in AD. Password hash sync affects cloud sign-in and gives Defender for Identity no rights to act in AD.","Domain 2: Defender for Identity response actions",["Device groups scope Defender for Endpoint; they give Defender for Identity no rights to change AD accounts.",null,"Password hash sync affects cloud authentication and gives Defender for Identity no rights in AD.","Storing a domain admin password for a playbook is risky and is not how Defender for Identity acts."],2],["x48",0,3,"A hunter has 50 suspicious tool names and wants to match any of them as whole terms anywhere in ProcessCommandLine. Which operator fits best?",["in","has_any","contains","=="],1,"has_any matches whole terms from a list anywhere in a string and uses the term index, so it is fast. in only matches when the whole column value equals one list item, so it misses longer command lines.","Domain 3: KQL string operators",["in matches only when the whole value equals a list item, so long command lines are missed.",null,"contains matches substrings and does not take a list, so it would need 50 separate conditions and runs slower.","== is an exact equality test on the whole value and takes only one value."],2],["x49",0,3,"A column holds a dynamic array of IP addresses per row. The hunter wants one row per IP address so they can join it to network logs. Which operator should be used?",["project-away","summarize make_set()","mv-expand","top-nested"],2,"mv-expand turns each array element into its own row. make_set does the opposite, gathering values into an array.","Domain 3: KQL dynamic data",["project-away removes columns; it does not split arrays into rows.","make_set gathers values into an array, the opposite of what is needed.",null,"top-nested builds hierarchical top-N aggregations; it does not expand arrays."],1],["x50",0,3,"A hunter wants to search both DeviceProcessEvents and DeviceFileEvents for a SHA-256 hash in one result set. Which operator combines the rows of the two tables?",["union","join kind=inner","lookup","evaluate bag_unpack"],0,"union appends rows from several tables into one result. join matches rows on keys, so it would only return rows where both tables share a key.","Domain 3: KQL union",[null,"An inner join matches rows on keys and returns only matches, not all rows from both tables.","lookup enriches rows from a fact table with a dimension table; it does not append rows.","bag_unpack spreads a dynamic property bag into columns; it does not combine tables."],1],["x51",0,3,"A hunter joins logon events (left) to process events (right) on DeviceId with a plain join, and many left rows that share a DeviceId seem to be missing. What explains this?",["join always drops rows with null values","The default join kind removes duplicate left keys before matching","join returns only the first 10,000 rows","The right table must be sorted by time"],1,"The default join kind is innerunique, which keeps only one left row per key before matching; kind=inner keeps them all. Nulls are not the issue here because the rows have DeviceId values.","Domain 3: KQL join kinds",["join does not simply drop rows with nulls; these rows all have DeviceId values.",null,"There is no fixed 10,000-row limit on join results that would explain the missing rows.","join does not need either table sorted by time."],3],["x52",0,3,"The hunter wants onboarded devices from an inventory list that had no DeviceNetworkEvents in the last 7 days. Which join kind returns rows from the left side with no match on the right?",["inner","leftouter","rightsemi","leftanti"],3,"leftanti returns left rows with no match on the right, which is exactly the devices with no events. leftouter returns all left rows, with and without matches, so the hunter would still need to filter.","Domain 3: KQL join kinds",["inner returns only rows that match on both sides, which is the opposite of what is needed.","leftouter returns all left rows, matched or not, so extra filtering would be needed.","rightsemi returns right-side rows that have a match, not left rows without one.",null],2],["x53",0,3,"The hunter needs the most recent logon record per device, with all its columns. Which KQL expression fits best?",["summarize max(Timestamp) by DeviceId","take 1 by DeviceId","summarize arg_max(Timestamp, *) by DeviceId","distinct DeviceId, Timestamp"],2,"arg_max(Timestamp, *) returns the whole row with the latest timestamp for each device. max(Timestamp) returns only the time value, losing the other columns.","Domain 3: KQL aggregation",["max(Timestamp) returns just the time value and loses the rest of the row's columns.","take does not support a by clause; it just returns a number of arbitrary rows.",null,"distinct returns every unique device and time pair, not only the latest per device."],2],["x54",0,3,"Which advanced hunting table records Active Directory changes seen by Defender for Identity, such as group membership changes and password resets?",["IdentityLogonEvents","IdentityDirectoryEvents","IdentityInfo","DeviceEvents"],1,"IdentityDirectoryEvents holds directory changes such as group membership changes and password changes. IdentityLogonEvents covers authentication activity, not changes to directory objects.","Domain 3: Advanced hunting schema",["IdentityLogonEvents records authentication activity, not changes to directory objects.",null,"IdentityInfo holds account details like department and title, not change events.","DeviceEvents holds miscellaneous endpoint events, not Active Directory changes seen by Defender for Identity."],1],["x55",0,3,"The hunter needs every alert, from any Defender product, that involved a particular SHA-256 hash, with each alert's title and severity. What is the best approach?",["Query DeviceFileEvents only","Query EmailAttachmentInfo only","Search the SecurityEvent table","Filter AlertEvidence on the hash and join to AlertInfo on AlertId"],3,"AlertEvidence lists entities attached to alerts, and AlertInfo holds titles and severity, joined on AlertId. DeviceFileEvents shows file activity on devices but not whether alerts were raised.","Domain 3: Advanced hunting alert tables",["DeviceFileEvents shows file activity on devices but not which alerts involved the hash.","EmailAttachmentInfo covers only email attachments, not alerts from every product.","SecurityEvent holds Windows Security events in Sentinel, not Defender alert evidence.",null],2],["x56",0,3,"A hunter needs the recipients of all delivered emails that contained a URL on the domain bad-example.test. Which approach fits?",["Query EmailUrlInfo for the domain and join EmailEvents on NetworkMessageId","Query UrlClickEvents only","Query DeviceNetworkEvents for the domain","Query EmailEvents alone using the Subject column"],0,"EmailUrlInfo holds URLs found in mail, and joining EmailEvents on NetworkMessageId adds recipients and delivery details. UrlClickEvents only shows clicks, so it misses recipients who never clicked.","Domain 3: Email hunting",[null,"UrlClickEvents records only clicks, so recipients who never clicked would be missed.","DeviceNetworkEvents shows device connections, not email recipients.","Subject text does not reveal the URLs in a message, so EmailEvents alone cannot answer this."],3],["x57",0,3,"A hunter wants to find users who deleted large numbers of files in SharePoint Online and OneDrive in a short time. Which advanced hunting table is the best starting point?",["DeviceFileEvents","EmailEvents","CloudAppEvents","IdentityQueryEvents"],2,"CloudAppEvents holds activity from cloud apps including SharePoint and OneDrive file operations. DeviceFileEvents covers files on onboarded endpoints, not in cloud services.","Domain 3: Advanced hunting schema",["DeviceFileEvents covers files on onboarded endpoints, not SharePoint or OneDrive operations.","EmailEvents covers email delivery, not file deletions.",null,"IdentityQueryEvents holds queries against Active Directory, not cloud file activity."],1],["x58",0,3,"A threat hunting lead wants to run a hypothesis-driven hunt in Sentinel, keeping its queries, bookmarks, status and findings together. Which feature fits?",["Hunts in Sentinel hunting","A workbook with pinned queries","A watchlist","An automation rule"],0,"Hunts let a team manage an end-to-end hunt, tracking hypothesis, queries, bookmarks and outcome in one place. A workbook visualizes data but does not track a hunt's progress.","Domain 3: Sentinel hunts",[null,"A workbook visualizes data but does not track a hunt's hypothesis, status and findings.","A watchlist is a reference list for queries, not a way to manage a hunt.","Automation rules act on incidents; they do not organize hunting work."],2],["x59",0,3,"The hunter needs full KQL, including joins across tables, over a specific two-day window of data that sits only in long-term retention. What should they use?",["A search job on the archived table","Livestream","A bookmark","Restore of that time range"],3,"Restore brings the chosen long-term data into interactive use so full KQL works on it. A search job is the tempting choice but it scans one table with a limited query and writes the results to a new table.","Domain 3: Long-term data",["A search job scans a single table with a limited query, so full KQL with joins is not possible.","Livestream watches new incoming events in real time, not old data in long-term retention.","A bookmark saves rows from a query result; it does not bring archived data back.",null],3],["x60",0,3,"Threat intelligence indicators are already in Sentinel. The SOC wants alerts when a firewall log in CommonSecurityLog contains a known-bad IP. What is the quickest way?",["Export indicators to a watchlist every day","Enable a built-in TI map analytics rule for IPs and CommonSecurityLog","Create a hunting bookmark for each indicator","Run a manual search job each week"],1,"The built-in TI map rule templates match indicators against common log tables and raise alerts automatically. A daily watchlist export duplicates data Sentinel already has and still needs a rule.","Domain 3: Threat intelligence matching",["A daily watchlist export copies data Sentinel already has and still needs a rule to alert.",null,"Bookmarks save hunting results; they do not match indicators automatically.","A weekly manual search is slow and not an alert."],2]]);
